CREATE TEMP TABLE _s495_testaments_xii_c (src_edition TEXT, src_slug TEXT, src_ch INT, src_v INT, src_text TEXT, tgt_edition TEXT, tgt_slug TEXT, tgt_ch INT, tgt_v INT, tgt_text TEXT, tier TEXT, note TEXT) ON COMMIT DROP;
INSERT INTO _s495_testaments_xii_c VALUES
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',2,4,$n$The first is the spirit of life, with which the constitution (of man) is created. The second is the sense of sight, with which ariseth desire.$n$,'canon','genesis',3,6,$n$And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:6 — *And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat.* *The first is the spirit of life, with which the constitution (of man) is created. The second is the sense of sight, with which ariseth desire.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 2:4) Reuben puts desire in the eye, and the oldest telling of it is in the garden, where the woman saw that the tree was pleasant to the eyes, and took. The Testament sorts the human frame into seven spirits and seven senses in a way Moses never does, so that arrangement is held loosely, but the road it describes, from seeing to wanting to taking, is the one Genesis had already walked.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',2,4,$n$The first is the spirit of life, with which the constitution (of man) is created. The second is the sense of sight, with which ariseth desire.$n$,'canon','job',31,1,$n$I made a covenant with mine eyes; why then should I think upon a maid?$n$,'free',$n$Job 31:1 — *I made a covenant with mine eyes; why then should I think upon a maid?* *The first is the spirit of life, with which the constitution (of man) is created. The second is the sense of sight, with which ariseth desire.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 2:4) Job answers the same danger with a decision instead of a list of spirits: he made a covenant with his eyes before the question could even rise. Reuben, who is speaking out of his own repentance, is warning his sons in that same direction, toward guarding the gate of the eye.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',2,9,$n$Wherefore it is the last in order of creation, and the first in that of youth, because it is filled with ignorance, and leadeth the youth as a blind man to a pit, and as a beast to a precipice.$n$,'canon','proverbs',7,22,$n$He goeth after her straightway, as an ox goeth to the slaughter, or as a fool to the correction of the stocks;$n$,'free',$n$Proverbs 7:22 — *He goeth after her straightway, as an ox goeth to the slaughter, or as a fool to the correction of the stocks;* *Wherefore it is the last in order of creation, and the first in that of youth, because it is filled with ignorance, and leadeth the youth as a blind man to a pit, and as a beast to a precipice.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 2:9) The Testament says desire leads the young man like a blind man to a pit and like a beast to a precipice. Proverbs paints the same road one street at a time, a young man following straight after her like an ox to the slaughter, and verse 23 adds that he goes on *and knoweth not that it is for his life*.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',7,2,$n$And they placed him in a coffin until they carried him up from Egypt, and buried him in Hebron in the cave where his father was.$n$,'canon','genesis',50,26,$n$So Joseph died, being an hundred and ten years old: and they embalmed him, and he was put in a coffin in Egypt.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 50:26 — *So Joseph died, being an hundred and ten years old: and they embalmed him, and he was put in a coffin in Egypt.* *And they placed him in a coffin until they carried him up from Egypt, and buried him in Hebron in the cave where his father was.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 7:2) Reuben's sons lay him in a coffin and keep him there until they can carry him up from Egypt, just as Joseph was put in a coffin in Egypt after making the sons of Yashar'el (Israel) swear *ye shall carry up my bones from hence* (Genesis 50:25). The generation that died in Egypt died with its face toward the land.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',7,2,$n$And they placed him in a coffin until they carried him up from Egypt, and buried him in Hebron in the cave where his father was.$n$,'canon','genesis',50,13,$n$For his sons carried him into the land of Canaan, and buried him in the cave of the field of Machpelah, which Abraham bought with the field for a possession of a buryingplace of Ephron the Hittite, before Mamre.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 50:13 — *For his sons carried him into the land of Canaan, and buried him in the cave of the field of Machpelah, which Abraham bought with the field for a possession of a buryingplace of Ephron the Hittite, before Mamre.* *And they placed him in a coffin until they carried him up from Egypt, and buried him in Hebron in the cave where his father was.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 7:2) The Testament says Reuben was buried in Hebron in the cave where his father lay, and Genesis names that cave: the field of Machpelah, which Abraham bought, where Jacob's sons buried their father. What the Testament adds about Reuben's own burial goes beyond what Genesis tells, and nothing here is built on it.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',8,2,$n$For when Simeon was sick, his sons came to visit him, and he strengthened himself and sat up and kissed them, and said:—$n$,'canon','genesis',48,2,$n$And one told Jacob, and said, Behold, thy son Joseph cometh unto thee: and Yashar'el (Israel) strengthened himself, and sat upon the bed.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 48:2 — *And one told Jacob, and said, Behold, thy son Joseph cometh unto thee: and Yashar'el (Israel) strengthened himself, and sat upon the bed.* *For when Simeon was sick, his sons came to visit him, and he strengthened himself and sat up and kissed them, and said:—* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 8:2) Simeon sits up on his sickbed and kisses his sons, and the picture is the one Genesis gives of Jacob when Joseph came to him: he strengthened himself and sat upon the bed. In both the dying man gathers what strength he has left so that his last words can be heard.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',8,1,$n$The copy of the words of Simeon, the things which he spake to his sons before he died, in the hundred and twentieth year of his life, at which time Joseph, his brother, died.$n$,'canon','genesis',50,26,$n$So Joseph died, being an hundred and ten years old: and they embalmed him, and he was put in a coffin in Egypt.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 50:26 — *So Joseph died, being an hundred and ten years old: and they embalmed him, and he was put in a coffin in Egypt.* *The copy of the words of Simeon, the things which he spake to his sons before he died, in the hundred and twentieth year of his life, at which time Joseph, his brother, died.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 8:1) The Testament dates Simeon's last words to the year Joseph died, and Genesis closes Joseph's life with the same death and the coffin in Egypt. Simeon, who once hated Joseph, speaks his farewell in the year the brother he wronged is laid to rest.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',16,2,$n$And they were in Egypt until the day of their departure by the hand of Moses.$n$,'canon','psalms',77,20,$n$Thou leddest thy people like a flock by the hand of Moses and Aaron.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 77:20 — *Thou leddest thy people like a flock by the hand of Moses and Aaron.* *And they were in Egypt until the day of their departure by the hand of Moses.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 16:2) Simeon's sons stay in Egypt until the day of their departure *by the hand of Moses*, and the psalm uses almost the same words when it remembers the exodus as a flock led out by the hand of Moses and Aaron.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',16,2,$n$And they were in Egypt until the day of their departure by the hand of Moses.$n$,'canon','exodus',12,41,$n$And it came to pass at the end of the four hundred and thirty years, even the selfsame day it came to pass, that all the hosts of Yahuah (LORD) went out from the land of Egypt.$n$,'free',$n$Exodus 12:41 — *And it came to pass at the end of the four hundred and thirty years, even the selfsame day it came to pass, that all the hosts of Yahuah (LORD) went out from the land of Egypt.* *And they were in Egypt until the day of their departure by the hand of Moses.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 16:2) The day of departure is the day Exodus fixes, at the end of the four hundred and thirty years, *the selfsame day* that all the hosts of Yahuah (the LORD) went out of Egypt. Simeon's sons are the generations waiting for that day.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',17,1,$n$The copy of the words of Levi, the things which he ordained unto his sons, according to all that they should do, and what things should befall them until the day of judgment.$n$,'canon','genesis',49,1,$n$And Jacob called unto his sons, and said, Gather yourselves together, that I may tell you that which shall befall you in the last days.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 49:1 — *And Jacob called unto his sons, and said, Gather yourselves together, that I may tell you that which shall befall you in the last days.* *The copy of the words of Levi, the things which he ordained unto his sons, according to all that they should do, and what things should befall them until the day of judgment.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 17:1) Levi gathers his sons to ordain what they are to do and what will befall them until the day of judgment, in the pattern of Jacob, who called his sons together to tell them what would befall them in the last days. A father's last word is a word about what is coming.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',17,2,$n$He was sound in health when he called them to him; for it had been revealed to him that he should die. And when they were gathered together he said to them:$n$,'canon','2-peter',1,14,$n$Knowing that shortly I must put off this my tabernacle, even as our Lord Yahusha HaMashiach (Lord Jesus Christ) hath shewed me.$n$,'free',$n$2 Peter 1:14 — *Knowing that shortly I must put off this my tabernacle, even as our Lord Yahusha HaMashiach (Lord Jesus Christ) hath shewed me.* *He was sound in health when he called them to him; for it had been revealed to him that he should die. And when they were gathered together he said to them:* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 17:2) Levi was sound in health when he called his sons, because it had been revealed to him that he should die. Peter says the same of himself: he knew that shortly he must put off his tabernacle, as it had been shewed him, and so he spoke while he could.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',23,3,$n$Because also they had wrought folly in Yashar'el (Israel) by defiling my sister. And we departed and came to Bethel.$n$,'canon','genesis',34,7,$n$And the sons of Jacob came out of the field when they heard it: and the men were grieved, and they were very wroth, because he had wrought folly in Yashar'el (Israel) in lying with Jacob’s daughter; which thing ought not to be done.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 34:7 — *And the sons of Jacob came out of the field when they heard it: and the men were grieved, and they were very wroth, because he had wrought folly in Yashar'el (Israel) in lying with Jacob’s daughter; which thing ought not to be done.* *Because also they had wrought folly in Yashar'el (Israel) by defiling my sister. And we departed and came to Bethel.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 23:3) Levi calls the defiling of his sister *folly in Yashar'el (Israel)*, and that is Genesis's own phrase, spoken when the sons of Jacob came in from the field and heard what had been done. The words name the wrong done to Dinah as a wrong done to the whole house.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',23,2,$n$For from this day forward shall Shechem be called a city of imbeciles; for as a man mocketh a fool, so did we mock them,$n$,'canon','genesis',34,30,$n$And Jacob said to Simeon and Levi, Ye have troubled me to make me to stink among the inhabitants of the land, among the Canaanites and the Perizzites: and I being few in number, they shall gather themselves together against me, and slay me; and I shall be destroyed, I and my house.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 34:30 — *And Jacob said to Simeon and Levi, Ye have troubled me to make me to stink among the inhabitants of the land, among the Canaanites and the Perizzites: and I being few in number, they shall gather themselves together against me, and slay me; and I shall be destroyed, I and my house.* *For from this day forward shall Shechem be called a city of imbeciles; for as a man mocketh a fool, so did we mock them,* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 23:2) Levi tells how they mocked the Shechemites, and leaves it there. Genesis keeps Jacob's answer: he told Simeon and Levi they had troubled him and put his whole house in danger, and at the end of his life he said *Cursed be their anger, for it was fierce* (Genesis 49:7). The two voices stand side by side in scripture and the reader is allowed to hear both.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',23,1,$n$And I said to my father Jacob: By thee will Yahuah (Lord) destroy the Canaanites, and will give their land to thee and to thy seed after thee.$n$,'canon','genesis',35,12,$n$And the land which I gave Abraham and Isaac, to thee I will give it, and to thy seed after thee will I give the land.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 35:12 — *And the land which I gave Abraham and Isaac, to thee I will give it, and to thy seed after thee will I give the land.* *And I said to my father Jacob: By thee will Yahuah (Lord) destroy the Canaanites, and will give their land to thee and to thy seed after thee.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 23:1) Levi tells his father that Yahuah (the Lord) will give the land to him and to his seed after him, and that is the promise Elohim (God) renewed to Jacob at Beth-el: the land given to Abraham and Isaac, to thee and to thy seed after thee. Levi is speaking as one standing in the line of the promise.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',27,8,$n$And Jochebed was born in Egypt, in my sixty-fourth year, for I was renowned then in the midst of my brethren.$n$,'canon','numbers',26,59,$n$And the name of Amram’s wife was Jochebed, the daughter of Levi, whom her mother bare to Levi in Egypt: and she bare unto Amram Aaron and Moses, and Miriam their sister.$n$,'free',$n$Numbers 26:59 — *And the name of Amram’s wife was Jochebed, the daughter of Levi, whom her mother bare to Levi in Egypt: and she bare unto Amram Aaron and Moses, and Miriam their sister.* *And Jochebed was born in Egypt, in my sixty-fourth year, for I was renowned then in the midst of my brethren.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 27:8) Levi says Jochebed was born in Egypt in his sixty-fourth year, and Numbers records the same birth in its own words: she was the daughter of Levi, whom her mother bare to Levi in Egypt, and she bare Aaron, Moses, and Miriam. The Testament supplies the year that Numbers leaves unsaid.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',27,2,$n$And she conceived and bare a son, and I called his name Gersam, for we were sojourners in our land.$n$,'canon','exodus',2,22,$n$And she bare him a son, and he called his name Gershom: for he said, I have been a stranger in a strange land.$n$,'free',$n$Exodus 2:22 — *And she bare him a son, and he called his name Gershom: for he said, I have been a stranger in a strange land.* *And she conceived and bare a son, and I called his name Gersam, for we were sojourners in our land.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 27:2) Levi gives Gersam his name because they were sojourners, and scripture gives that same reason for the same kind of name when Moses names his own son in Midian. The name Gersam, which the lists of the sons of Levi spell Gershon, is the name of a stranger in a strange land.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',27,5,$n$And I saw in a vision that he was standing on high in the midst of all the congregation.$n$,'canon','numbers',3,31,$n$And their charge shall be the ark, and the table, and the candlestick, and the altars, and the vessels of the sanctuary wherewith they minister, and the hanging, and all the service thereof.$n$,'free',$n$Numbers 3:31 — *And their charge shall be the ark, and the table, and the candlestick, and the altars, and the vessels of the sanctuary wherewith they minister, and the hanging, and all the service thereof.* *And I saw in a vision that he was standing on high in the midst of all the congregation.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 27:5) Levi sees in a vision Kohath standing on high in the midst of the congregation, and Numbers gives that standing its shape: the charge of the sons of Kohath was the ark, the table, the candlestick, the altars, and the vessels of the sanctuary. The vision and the charge fit together.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',28,2,$n$And the sons of Kohath, Ambram, Issachar, Hebron, and Ozeel.$n$,'canon','exodus',6,18,$n$And the sons of Kohath; Amram, and Izhar, and Hebron, and Uzziel: and the years of the life of Kohath were an hundred thirty and three years.$n$,'free',$n$Exodus 6:18 — *And the sons of Kohath; Amram, and Izhar, and Hebron, and Uzziel: and the years of the life of Kohath were an hundred thirty and three years.* *And the sons of Kohath, Ambram, Issachar, Hebron, and Ozeel.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 28:2) Levi lists the four sons of Kohath as Ambram, Issachar, Hebron, and Ozeel, and Exodus lists the same four by the spellings that stand in the canon: Amram, Izhar, Hebron, and Uzziel. The names are spelled a little differently but the family is the same.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',28,4,$n$And in my ninety-fourth year Ambram took Jochebed my daughter to him to wife, for they were born in one day, he and my daughter.$n$,'canon','exodus',6,20,$n$And Amram took him Jochebed his father’s sister to wife; and she bare him Aaron and Moses: and the years of the life of Amram were an hundred and thirty and seven years.$n$,'free',$n$Exodus 6:20 — *And Amram took him Jochebed his father’s sister to wife; and she bare him Aaron and Moses: and the years of the life of Amram were an hundred and thirty and seven years.* *And in my ninety-fourth year Ambram took Jochebed my daughter to him to wife, for they were born in one day, he and my daughter.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 28:4) Ambram takes Jochebed, Levi's daughter, to wife, and Exodus says that Amram took Jochebed and she bare him Aaron and Moses. The marriage that brought Moses and Aaron into the world was a marriage inside the house of Levi.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',28,6,$n$And behold, my children, ye are a third generation.$n$,'canon','genesis',15,16,$n$But in the fourth generation they shall come hither again: for the iniquity of the Amorites is not yet full.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 15:16 — *But in the fourth generation they shall come hither again: for the iniquity of the Amorites is not yet full.* *And behold, my children, ye are a third generation.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 28:6) Levi tells his sons *ye are a third generation*, and a man who keeps his years and the births of his sons this carefully is a man counting down a promise. Genesis had said that in the fourth generation they would come back, because the iniquity of the Amorites was not yet full.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',31,1,$n$Therefore the temple, which Yahuah (Lord) shall choose, shall be laid waste through your uncleanness, and ye shall be captives throughout all nations.$n$,'canon','ezekiel',5,11,$n$Wherefore, as I live, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Surely, because thou hast defiled my sanctuary with all thy detestable things, and with all thine abominations, therefore will I also diminish thee; neither shall mine eye spare, neither will I have any pity.$n$,'free',$n$Ezekiel 5:11 — *Wherefore, as I live, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Surely, because thou hast defiled my sanctuary with all thy detestable things, and with all thine abominations, therefore will I also diminish thee; neither shall mine eye spare, neither will I have any pity.* *Therefore the temple, which Yahuah (Lord) shall choose, shall be laid waste through your uncleanness, and ye shall be captives throughout all nations.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 31:1) Levi says the temple will be laid waste through the uncleanness of the sons, and Ezekiel gives Yahuah's (the Lord's) own reason for the same ruin: the sanctuary had been defiled with detestable things. The cause named is the defiling, never the temple and never the law that taught them what was clean.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',31,2,$n$And ye shall be an abomination unto them, and ye shall receive reproach and everlasting shame from the righteous judgment of Elohim (God).$n$,'canon','deuteronomy',28,37,$n$And thou shalt become an astonishment, a proverb, and a byword, among all nations whither Yahuah (LORD) shall lead thee.$n$,'free',$n$Deuteronomy 28:37 — *And thou shalt become an astonishment, a proverb, and a byword, among all nations whither Yahuah (LORD) shall lead thee.* *And ye shall be an abomination unto them, and ye shall receive reproach and everlasting shame from the righteous judgment of Elohim (God).* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 31:2) Levi's word that they will be an abomination among the nations is the warning Moses set down in the curses, that the scattering would make them an astonishment, a proverb, and a byword. That curse is the judgment that follows turning away from the commandments, not the commandments themselves.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',31,4,$n$And if you were not to receive mercy through Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, our fathers, not one of our seed should be left upon the earth.$n$,'canon','leviticus',26,42,$n$Then will I remember my covenant with Jacob, and also my covenant with Isaac, and also my covenant with Abraham will I remember; and I will remember the land.$n$,'free',$n$Leviticus 26:42 — *Then will I remember my covenant with Jacob, and also my covenant with Isaac, and also my covenant with Abraham will I remember; and I will remember the land.* *And if you were not to receive mercy through Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, our fathers, not one of our seed should be left upon the earth.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 31:4) Levi's reason for the mercy, Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, our fathers, is the reason Yahuah (the LORD) gave in the Torah long before the scattering: even in the land of their enemies he would remember his covenant with Jacob, Isaac, and Abraham, and the land.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',31,4,$n$And if you were not to receive mercy through Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, our fathers, not one of our seed should be left upon the earth.$n$,'canon','isaiah',1,9,$n$Except Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts) had left unto us a very small remnant, we should have been as Sodom, and we should have been like unto Gomorrah.$n$,'free',$n$Isaiah 1:9 — *Except Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts) had left unto us a very small remnant, we should have been as Sodom, and we should have been like unto Gomorrah.* *And if you were not to receive mercy through Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, our fathers, not one of our seed should be left upon the earth.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 31:4) Levi says that without mercy for the fathers' sake not one of the seed would be left upon the earth, and Isaiah says it as a remnant: except Yahuah Tseva'ot (the LORD of hosts) had left a very small remnant, they would have been as Sodom.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',33,1,$n$And whereas ye have heard concerning the seventy weeks, hear also concerning the priesthood.$n$,'canon','daniel',9,24,$n$Seventy weeks are determined upon thy people and upon thy holy city, to finish the transgression, and to make an end of sins, and to make reconciliation for iniquity, and to bring in everlasting righteousness, and to seal up the vision and prophecy, and to anoint the most Holy.$n$,'free',$n$Daniel 9:24 — *Seventy weeks are determined upon thy people and upon thy holy city, to finish the transgression, and to make an end of sins, and to make reconciliation for iniquity, and to bring in everlasting righteousness, and to seal up the vision and prophecy, and to anoint the most Holy.* *And whereas ye have heard concerning the seventy weeks, hear also concerning the priesthood.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 33:1) Levi speaks of the seventy weeks and goes straight on to the priesthood, as Daniel's seventy weeks are set upon his people and his holy city and end with the anointing of the most Holy. The Testament lays its own scheme of jubilees and priests over the weeks, and Daniel's verse is read here only as Daniel.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',33,2,$n$For in each jubilee there shall be a priesthood. And in the first jubilee, the first who is anointed to the priesthood shall be great, and shall speak to Elohim (God) as to a father. And his priesthood shall be perfect with Yahuah (Lord), [and in the day of his gladness shall he arise for the salvation of the world].$n$,'canon','malachi',2,6,$n$The law of truth was in his mouth, and iniquity was not found in his lips: he walked with me in peace and equity, and did turn many away from iniquity.$n$,'free',$n$Malachi 2:6 — *The law of truth was in his mouth, and iniquity was not found in his lips: he walked with me in peace and equity, and did turn many away from iniquity.* *For in each jubilee there shall be a priesthood. And in the first jubilee, the first who is anointed to the priesthood shall be great, and shall speak to Elohim (God) as to a father. And his priesthood shall be perfect with Yahuah (Lord), [and in the day of his gladness shall he arise for the salvation of the world].* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 33:2) Of the first priest Levi says he speaks to Elohim (God) as to a father and that his priesthood is perfect with Yahuah (the Lord). Malachi describes the true priest in the same way, one in whose mouth was the law of truth, who walked with Yahuah in peace and equity. The bracketed words at the end of Levi's verse are set off in the text as an addition, and this note rests only on what comes before them.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',33,10,$n$And in the fifth week they shall return to their desolate country, and shall renew the house of Yahuah (Lord).$n$,'canon','ezra',1,5,$n$Then rose up the chief of the fathers of Yahudah (Judah) and Benjamin, and the priests, and the Levites, with all them whose spirit Elohim (God) had raised, to go up to build the house of Yahuah (LORD) which is in Jerusalem.$n$,'free',$n$Ezra 1:5 — *Then rose up the chief of the fathers of Yahudah (Judah) and Benjamin, and the priests, and the Levites, with all them whose spirit Elohim (God) had raised, to go up to build the house of Yahuah (LORD) which is in Jerusalem.* *And in the fifth week they shall return to their desolate country, and shall renew the house of Yahuah (Lord).* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 33:10) The return in the fifth week to the desolate country, to renew the house of Yahuah (the Lord), is the return Ezra records, when the chief of the fathers of Yahudah (Judah) and Benjamin, with the priests and Levites, rose up to build the house in Jerusalem.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',33,11,$n$And in the seventh week shall come priests, (who are) idolaters, adulterers, lovers of money, proud, lawless, lascivious, abusers of children and beasts.$n$,'canon','malachi',2,8,$n$But ye are departed out of the way; ye have caused many to stumble at the law; ye have corrupted the covenant of Levi, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts).$n$,'free',$n$Malachi 2:8 — *But ye are departed out of the way; ye have caused many to stumble at the law; ye have corrupted the covenant of Levi, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts).* *And in the seventh week shall come priests, (who are) idolaters, adulterers, lovers of money, proud, lawless, lascivious, abusers of children and beasts.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 33:11) Levi's seventh week brings priests who are idolaters, lovers of money, and lawless. Malachi charged the priests of his own day with the same departure: they had left the way and caused many to stumble at the law. The fault in both is the priests leaving the law, never the law.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',37,1,$n$And Yahuah (Lord) showed me favour in all my works both in the field and in the house.$n$,'canon','psalms',44,3,$n$For they got not the land in possession by their own sword, neither did their own arm save them: but thy right hand, and thine arm, and the light of thy countenance, because thou hadst a favour unto them.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 44:3 — *For they got not the land in possession by their own sword, neither did their own arm save them: but thy right hand, and thine arm, and the light of thy countenance, because thou hadst a favour unto them.* *And Yahuah (Lord) showed me favour in all my works both in the field and in the house.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 37:1) Yahudah (Judah) says Yahuah (the Lord) showed him favour in all his works, and the psalm says where all such strength comes from: not their own sword, not their own arm, but thy right hand, and thine arm, and the light of thy countenance, because thou hadst a favour unto them.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',37,4,$n$I slew a lion and plucked a kid out of its mouth. I took a bear by its paw and hurled it down the cliff, and it was crushed.$n$,'canon','1-samuel',17,37,$n$David said moreover, Yahuah (LORD) that delivered me out of the paw of the lion, and out of the paw of the bear, he will deliver me out of the hand of this Philistine. And Saul said unto David, Go, and Yahuah (LORD) be with thee.$n$,'free',$n$1 Samuel 17:37 — *David said moreover, Yahuah (LORD) that delivered me out of the paw of the lion, and out of the paw of the bear, he will deliver me out of the hand of this Philistine. And Saul said unto David, Go, and Yahuah (LORD) be with thee.* *I slew a lion and plucked a kid out of its mouth. I took a bear by its paw and hurled it down the cliff, and it was crushed.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 37:4) Yahudah (Judah) tells of taking a bear by the paw and slaying a lion, and David, facing Goliath, speaks of Yahuah (the LORD) who delivered him out of the paw of the lion and out of the paw of the bear. Both men tell of strength against the beasts, and David puts the credit where Yahudah (Judah) does in the first verse of this chapter, with Yahuah.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',37,4,$n$I slew a lion and plucked a kid out of its mouth. I took a bear by its paw and hurled it down the cliff, and it was crushed.$n$,'canon','genesis',49,9,$n$Yahudah (Judah) is a lion’s whelp: from the prey, my son, thou art gone up: he stooped down, he couched as a lion, and as an old lion; who shall rouse him up?$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 49:9 — *Yahudah (Judah) is a lion’s whelp: from the prey, my son, thou art gone up: he stooped down, he couched as a lion, and as an old lion; who shall rouse him up?* *I slew a lion and plucked a kid out of its mouth. I took a bear by its paw and hurled it down the cliff, and it was crushed.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 37:4) Jacob's blessing calls this son a lion's whelp, the very beast the Testament has him fighting. The boyhood tale and the father's blessing point at the same man.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',38,3,$n$Achor the king] a man of giant stature 'I found', hurling javelins before and behind as he sat on horseback, and I took up a stone of sixty pounds weight, and hurled it and smote his horse, and killed it.$n$,'jasher','jasher',37,32,$n$And Judah took up a large stone from the ground, and its weight was sixty shekels, and Judah ran toward Jashub, and with the stone struck him on his shield, that Jashub was stunned with the blow, and fell off from his horse to the ground.$n$,'free',$n$Jasher 37:32 — *And Judah took up a large stone from the ground, and its weight was sixty shekels, and Judah ran toward Jashub, and with the stone struck him on his shield, that Jashub was stunned with the blow, and fell off from his horse to the ground.* *Achor the king] a man of giant stature 'I found', hurling javelins before and behind as he sat on horseback, and I took up a stone of sixty pounds weight, and hurled it and smote his horse, and killed it.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 38:3) Yahudah (Judah) takes up a stone of sixty pounds weight and smites the giant's horse; Jasher tells the same kind of fight, with a stone of sixty shekels thrown by Judah at a king on horseback. The Testament and Jasher are telling one battle from two sides.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',38,7,$n$And Jacob my father slew 'Beelesath, king' of all the kings, a giant in strength, twelve cubits high.$n$,'canon','deuteronomy',3,11,$n$For only Og king of Bashan remained of the remnant of giants; behold, his bedstead was a bedstead of iron; is it not in Rabbath of the children of Ammon? nine cubits was the length thereof, and four cubits the breadth of it, after the cubit of a man.$n$,'free',$n$Deuteronomy 3:11 — *For only Og king of Bashan remained of the remnant of giants; behold, his bedstead was a bedstead of iron; is it not in Rabbath of the children of Ammon? nine cubits was the length thereof, and four cubits the breadth of it, after the cubit of a man.* *And Jacob my father slew 'Beelesath, king' of all the kings, a giant in strength, twelve cubits high.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 38:7) The Testament has a giant king twelve cubits high, taller than Og king of Bashan, whose iron bedstead was nine cubits long, and whom the Torah calls the last of the remnant of giants. The size in the Testament is the storyteller's, and the plain scripture is the one that sets the measure.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',38,10,$n$For he saw in a vision 'concerning me' that an angel of might followed me everywhere, that I should not be overcome.$n$,'canon','genesis',48,16,$n$The Angel which redeemed me from all evil, bless the lads; and let my name be named on them, and the name of my fathers Abraham and Isaac; and let them grow into a multitude in the midst of the earth.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 48:16 — *The Angel which redeemed me from all evil, bless the lads; and let my name be named on them, and the name of my fathers Abraham and Isaac; and let them grow into a multitude in the midst of the earth.* *For he saw in a vision 'concerning me' that an angel of might followed me everywhere, that I should not be overcome.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 38:10) Jacob dreams of an angel of might who followed Yahudah (Judah) everywhere so that he could not be overcome, and at the end of his life Jacob speaks of the Angel which redeemed him from all evil. The father who sees an angel following his son is the same who blessed with that Angel's name.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',39,1,$n$And in the south there came upon us a greater war than that in Shechem; and I joined in battle array with my brethren, and pursued a thousand men, and slew of them two hundred men and four kings.$n$,'canon','joshua',23,10,$n$One man of you shall chase a thousand: for Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God), he it is that fighteth for you, as he hath promised you.$n$,'free',$n$Joshua 23:10 — *One man of you shall chase a thousand: for Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God), he it is that fighteth for you, as he hath promised you.* *And in the south there came upon us a greater war than that in Shechem; and I joined in battle array with my brethren, and pursued a thousand men, and slew of them two hundred men and four kings.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 39:1) Yahudah (Judah) pursues a thousand men, and Joshua tells Yashar'el (Israel) that one man of them shall chase a thousand, and he says why: Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God), he it is that fighteth for you. The thousand put to flight is promised in scripture and is not credited to the man.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',39,2,$n$And I went up upon the wall, and I slew four mighty men.$n$,'jasher','jasher',38,14,$n$And the sons of Jacob came to the city and saw, and behold there was another wall to the city, and they sought for the gate of the wall and could not find it, and Judah sprang upon the top of the wall, and Simeon and Levi followed him, and they all three descended from the wall into the city.$n$,'free',$n$Jasher 38:14 — *And the sons of Jacob came to the city and saw, and behold there was another wall to the city, and they sought for the gate of the wall and could not find it, and Judah sprang upon the top of the wall, and Simeon and Levi followed him, and they all three descended from the wall into the city.* *And I went up upon the wall, and I slew four mighty men.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 39:2) Yahudah (Judah) goes up on the wall and slays four mighty men, and Jasher tells of the same leap: Judah sprang upon the top of the wall, and Simeon and Levi followed him and descended into the city. The wall in the Testament is a wall that Jasher remembers.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',39,3,$n$And so we captured Hazor, and took all the spoil.$n$,'canon','joshua',11,10,$n$And Joshua at that time turned back, and took Hazor, and smote the king thereof with the sword: for Hazor beforetime was the head of all those kingdoms.$n$,'free',$n$Joshua 11:10 — *And Joshua at that time turned back, and took Hazor, and smote the king thereof with the sword: for Hazor beforetime was the head of all those kingdoms.* *And so we captured Hazor, and took all the spoil.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 39:3) The Testament says they captured Hazor and took all the spoil, and Joshua says that Hazor beforetime was the head of all those kingdoms, and that Joshua took it and smote its king. The city bears the same name in both, and an age separates the two tellings; nothing here rests on settling them.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',40,3,$n$And they that were upon the wall, thinking that we were alone, 'were drawn down' against us.$n$,'canon','joshua',8,5,$n$And I, and all the people that are with me, will approach unto the city: and it shall come to pass, when they come out against us, as at the first, that we will flee before them,$n$,'free',$n$Joshua 8:5 — *And I, and all the people that are with me, will approach unto the city: and it shall come to pass, when they come out against us, as at the first, that we will flee before them,* *And they that were upon the wall, thinking that we were alone, 'were drawn down' against us.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 40:3) The men on the wall are drawn down against the brothers, thinking they are alone, while the others climb in behind. At Ai, Joshua told his people to flee before the men of the city until the city was emptied and the ambush could rise: the same plan of drawing the defenders out.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',40,5,$n$And we took it with the edge of the sword. And as for those who had taken refuge in the tower, we set fire to the tower and took both it and them.$n$,'canon','joshua',8,8,$n$And it shall be, when ye have taken the city, that ye shall set the city on fire: according to the commandment of Yahuah (LORD) shall ye do. See, I have commanded you.$n$,'free',$n$Joshua 8:8 — *And it shall be, when ye have taken the city, that ye shall set the city on fire: according to the commandment of Yahuah (LORD) shall ye do. See, I have commanded you.* *And we took it with the edge of the sword. And as for those who had taken refuge in the tower, we set fire to the tower and took both it and them.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 40:5) The brothers set fire to the tower and take both it and the men in it. In scripture a city set on fire was done at a word from Yahuah (the LORD), and the Testament tells the burning of its tower without claiming one; the reader can see the difference.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',40,2,$n$But I and Gad approached on the east side of the city, and Reuben and Levi on the west.$n$,'jasher','jasher',38,30,$n$And the number of the people that were upon the wall was about four hundred men, and when the sons of Jacob saw that the men of the city would not let them open the gates of the city, they sprang and ascended the top of the wall, and Judah went up first to the east part of the city.$n$,'free',$n$Jasher 38:30 — *And the number of the people that were upon the wall was about four hundred men, and when the sons of Jacob saw that the men of the city would not let them open the gates of the city, they sprang and ascended the top of the wall, and Judah went up first to the east part of the city.* *But I and Gad approached on the east side of the city, and Reuben and Levi on the west.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 40:2) Yahudah (Judah) tells of dividing the approach, himself and Gad on the east and Reuben and Levi on the west. Jasher tells of the same kind of assault on a walled city, with the brothers going up on different sides and Judah first on the east.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',40,6,$n$And as we were departing the men of Tappuah seized our spoil, and seeing (this) we fought with them.$n$,'jasher','jasher',39,2,$n$So all that remained in the city of Tapnach came out to fight with the sons of Jacob, and they thought to retake from them the booty and the spoil which they had captured from Chazar and Sarton.$n$,'free',$n$Jasher 39:2 — *So all that remained in the city of Tapnach came out to fight with the sons of Jacob, and they thought to retake from them the booty and the spoil which they had captured from Chazar and Sarton.* *And as we were departing the men of Tappuah seized our spoil, and seeing (this) we fought with them.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 40:6) The men of Tappuah seize the spoil as the brothers depart, and Jasher tells how all who remained in the city of Tapnach came out to retake the spoil that had been captured from Chazar and Sarton.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',41,1,$n$And when I was at the waters of Kozeba, the men of 'Jobel' came against us to battle.$n$,'canon','genesis',38,5,$n$And she yet again conceived, and bare a son; and called his name Shelah: and he was at Chezib, when she bare him.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 38:5 — *And she yet again conceived, and bare a son; and called his name Shelah: and he was at Chezib, when she bare him.* *And when I was at the waters of Kozeba, the men of 'Jobel' came against us to battle.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 41:1) Yahudah (Judah) fights at the waters of Kozeba, and Genesis says that his son Shelah was born at Chezib, which appears to be the same place by another spelling; the descendants of Shelah are later called *the men of Chozeba* (1 Chronicles 4:22). The place is a Judahite place in both tellings.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',41,4,$n$And when we came to their city their women rolled upon us stones from the brow of the hill on which the city stood.$n$,'canon','2-samuel',11,21,$n$Who smote Abimelech the son of Jerubbesheth? did not a woman cast a piece of a millstone upon him from the wall, that he died in Thebez? why went ye nigh the wall? then say thou, Thy servant Uriah the Hittite is dead also.$n$,'free',$n$2 Samuel 11:21 — *Who smote Abimelech the son of Jerubbesheth? did not a woman cast a piece of a millstone upon him from the wall, that he died in Thebez? why went ye nigh the wall? then say thou, Thy servant Uriah the Hittite is dead also.* *And when we came to their city their women rolled upon us stones from the brow of the hill on which the city stood.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 41:4) The women of the city roll stones down on the brothers from the hill, and scripture knows what a stone from above can do to a man who comes near a wall: a woman cast a piece of a millstone upon Abimelech from the wall at Thebez.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',41,4,$n$And when we came to their city their women rolled upon us stones from the brow of the hill on which the city stood.$n$,'jasher','jasher',39,9,$n$And the sons of Jacob fought with them in the city, and all their women were engaged in slinging against the sons of Jacob, and the combat was very severe amongst them the whole of that day till evening.$n$,'free',$n$Jasher 39:9 — *And the sons of Jacob fought with them in the city, and all their women were engaged in slinging against the sons of Jacob, and the combat was very severe amongst them the whole of that day till evening.* *And when we came to their city their women rolled upon us stones from the brow of the hill on which the city stood.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 41:4) Jasher tells the same thing of Arbelan, where all their women were engaged in slinging against the sons of Jacob and the fight was severe the whole of that day.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',42,2,$n$I, therefore, and Dan feigned ourselves to be Amorites, and as allies went into their city.$n$,'canon','joshua',9,4,$n$They did work wilily, and went and made as if they had been ambassadors, and took old sacks upon their asses, and wine bottles, old, and rent, and bound up;$n$,'free',$n$Joshua 9:4 — *They did work wilily, and went and made as if they had been ambassadors, and took old sacks upon their asses, and wine bottles, old, and rent, and bound up;* *I, therefore, and Dan feigned ourselves to be Amorites, and as allies went into their city.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 42:2) Yahudah (Judah) and Dan feign themselves to be Amorites and are let in as allies. Scripture tells of a pretending of this kind from the other side: the Gibeonites worked wilily and made as if they had been ambassadors, and it was the men of Yashar'el (Israel) who were taken in. The Testament tells the trick as a victory, and the reader can set it beside the Gibeonite one.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',42,9,$n$And I built Thamna, and my father built Rabael.$n$,'jubilees','jubilees',34,8,$n$And he prevailed over them, and imposed tribute on them that they should pay him tribute, five fruit products of their land, and he built Rôbêl and Tamnâtârês$n$,'free',$n$Jubilees 34:8 — *And he prevailed over them, and imposed tribute on them that they should pay him tribute, five fruit products of their land, and he built Rôbêl and Tamnâtârês* *And I built Thamna, and my father built Rabael.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 42:9) Yahudah (Judah) says he built Thamna and his father built Rabael, and Jubilees knows the same two places, Robel and Tamnatares, built at the end of the war with the kings of the Amorites. Jubilees gives the building to Jacob where the Testament shares it between father and son.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',42,11,$n$And the Canaanites feared me and my brethren.$n$,'canon','genesis',35,5,$n$And they journeyed: and the terror of Elohim (God) was upon the cities that were round about them, and they did not pursue after the sons of Jacob.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 35:5 — *And they journeyed: and the terror of Elohim (God) was upon the cities that were round about them, and they did not pursue after the sons of Jacob.* *And the Canaanites feared me and my brethren.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 42:11) The Canaanites feared Yahudah (Judah) and his brothers, and Genesis says why: the terror of Elohim (God) was upon the cities round about, and they did not pursue after the sons of Jacob. The fear is Elohim's, not the brothers'.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',42,1,$n$And the next day it was told us that the king of the city of Gaash with a mighty host was coming against us.$n$,'jasher','jasher',39,14,$n$And on the fifth day the sons of Jacob heard that the people of Gaash had gathered against them to battle, because they had slain their king and their captains, for there had been fourteen captains in the city of Gaash, and the sons of Jacob had slain them all in the first battle.$n$,'free',$n$Jasher 39:14 — *And on the fifth day the sons of Jacob heard that the people of Gaash had gathered against them to battle, because they had slain their king and their captains, for there had been fourteen captains in the city of Gaash, and the sons of Jacob had slain them all in the first battle.* *And the next day it was told us that the king of the city of Gaash with a mighty host was coming against us.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 42:1) The king of Gaash comes against them with a mighty host, and Jasher tells that on the fifth day the sons of Jacob heard that the people of Gaash had gathered against them to battle, because they had slain their king and their captains. The Testament and Jasher both begin the day at Gaash.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',43,2,$n$And when I went to him I saw Barsaba, king of Adullam; 'and he spake unto us', and he made us a feast; and when I was heated he gave me his daughter Bathshua to wife.$n$,'canon','genesis',38,2,$n$And Yahudah (Judah) saw there a daughter of a certain Canaanite, whose name was Shuah; and he took her, and went in unto her.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 38:2 — *And Yahudah (Judah) saw there a daughter of a certain Canaanite, whose name was Shuah; and he took her, and went in unto her.* *And when I went to him I saw Barsaba, king of Adullam; 'and he spake unto us', and he made us a feast; and when I was heated he gave me his daughter Bathshua to wife.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 43:2) The Testament says Barsaba, king of Adullam, made a feast and gave Yahudah (Judah) his daughter Bathshua; Genesis says Yahudah (Judah) saw there a daughter of a certain Canaanite, Shuah, and took her. The two tellings differ on how the match was made, and Genesis is the simpler account.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',43,2,$n$And when I went to him I saw Barsaba, king of Adullam; 'and he spake unto us', and he made us a feast; and when I was heated he gave me his daughter Bathshua to wife.$n$,'canon','genesis',28,1,$n$And Isaac called Jacob, and blessed him, and charged him, and said unto him, Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Canaan.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 28:1 — *And Isaac called Jacob, and blessed him, and charged him, and said unto him, Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Canaan.* *And when I went to him I saw Barsaba, king of Adullam; 'and he spake unto us', and he made us a feast; and when I was heated he gave me his daughter Bathshua to wife.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 43:2) Yahudah (Judah) marries a Canaanite's daughter in this verse, and Isaac's charge to Jacob stands beside it in scripture: Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Canaan. The Testament comes back to this matter itself in a later chapter.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',43,3,$n$She bare me Er, and Onan, and Shelah; and two of them Yahuah (Lord) smote: for Shelah lived, and his children are ye.$n$,'canon','1-chronicles',2,3,$n$The sons of Yahudah (Judah); Er, and Onan, and Shelah: which three were born unto him of the daughter of Shua the Canaanitess. And Er, the firstborn of Yahudah (Judah), was evil in the sight of Yahuah (LORD); and he slew him.$n$,'free',$n$1 Chronicles 2:3 — *The sons of Yahudah (Judah); Er, and Onan, and Shelah: which three were born unto him of the daughter of Shua the Canaanitess. And Er, the firstborn of Yahudah (Judah), was evil in the sight of Yahuah (LORD); and he slew him.* *She bare me Er, and Onan, and Shelah; and two of them Yahuah (Lord) smote: for Shelah lived, and his children are ye.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 43:3) Bathshua bears Er, Onan, and Shelah, and the Chronicles lists the same three sons, born of the daughter of Shua the Canaanitess, and says Er was evil in the sight of Yahuah (the LORD) and that he slew him. Two of the three were smitten, and Shelah lived.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',44,1,$n$And eighteen years my father abode at peace with his brother Esau, and his sons with us, after that we came from Mesopotamia, from Laban.$n$,'canon','genesis',35,29,$n$And Isaac gave up the ghost, and died, and was gathered unto his people, being old and full of days: and his sons Esau and Jacob buried him.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 35:29 — *And Isaac gave up the ghost, and died, and was gathered unto his people, being old and full of days: and his sons Esau and Jacob buried him.* *And eighteen years my father abode at peace with his brother Esau, and his sons with us, after that we came from Mesopotamia, from Laban.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 44:1) The Testament says Jacob abode at peace with Esau for eighteen years, and Genesis shows the two brothers together at the grave of their father: his sons Esau and Jacob buried him. The Testament, like Jubilees, tells what came after that peace broke.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',44,3,$n$And Jacob smote Esau with an arrow, and he was taken up wounded on Mount Seir, and as he went he died at Anoniram.$n$,'jubilees','jubilees',38,2,$n$Then Jacob bent his bow and sent forth the arrow and struck Esau, his brother, (on his right breast) and slew him.$n$,'free',$n$Jubilees 38:2 — *Then Jacob bent his bow and sent forth the arrow and struck Esau, his brother, (on his right breast) and slew him.* *And Jacob smote Esau with an arrow, and he was taken up wounded on Mount Seir, and as he went he died at Anoniram.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 44:3) Jacob smites Esau with an arrow, and Jubilees tells it in the same way: Jacob bent his bow, sent forth the arrow, and struck Esau his brother, and slew him. The two books remember one war.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',44,7,$n$Then they asked from us terms of peace; and having taken counsel with our father, we received them as tributaries.$n$,'jubilees','jubilees',38,12,$n$And Jacob sent word to his sons that they should make peace, and they made peace with them, and placed the yoke of servitude upon them, so that they paid tribute to Jacob and to his sons always.$n$,'free',$n$Jubilees 38:12 — *And Jacob sent word to his sons that they should make peace, and they made peace with them, and placed the yoke of servitude upon them, so that they paid tribute to Jacob and to his sons always.* *Then they asked from us terms of peace; and having taken counsel with our father, we received them as tributaries.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 44:7) The sons of Esau ask for terms and are received as tributaries; Jubilees says Jacob sent word to his sons that they should make peace, and they placed the yoke of servitude upon them so that they paid tribute to Jacob and to his sons always.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',62,15,$n$And Jacob knew Leah, and she 'conceived and' bare me, and on account of the hire I was called Issachar.$n$,'canon','genesis',30,18,$n$And Leah said, Elohim (God) hath given me my hire, because I have given my maiden to my husband: and she called his name Issachar.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 30:18 — *And Leah said, Elohim (God) hath given me my hire, because I have given my maiden to my husband: and she called his name Issachar.* *And Jacob knew Leah, and she 'conceived and' bare me, and on account of the hire I was called Issachar.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 62:15) Genesis gives the reason for the name in Leah's own mouth, and the Testament of Issachar has the son say the same thing back: "on account of the hire I was called Issachar." The name is a receipt for the night Leah paid for.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',62,3,$n$For Reuben 'my brother' brought in mandrakes from the field, and Rachel met him and took them.$n$,'canon','genesis',30,14,$n$And Reuben went in the days of wheat harvest, and found mandrakes in the field, and brought them unto his mother Leah. Then Rachel said to Leah, Give me, I pray thee, of thy son’s mandrakes.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 30:14 — *And Reuben went in the days of wheat harvest, and found mandrakes in the field, and brought them unto his mother Leah. Then Rachel said to Leah, Give me, I pray thee, of thy son’s mandrakes.* *For Reuben 'my brother' brought in mandrakes from the field, and Rachel met him and took them.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 62:3) Genesis puts Reuben in the field in the days of wheat harvest and has Rachel ask Leah for the mandrakes, while the Testament of Issachar has Rachel meet him and take them. The two tellings agree on where the quarrel between the sisters began.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',62,14,$n$Nevertheless, for the mandrakes I am hiring Jacob to thee for one night.$n$,'canon','genesis',30,16,$n$And Jacob came out of the field in the evening, and Leah went out to meet him, and said, Thou must come in unto me; for surely I have hired thee with my son’s mandrakes. And he lay with her that night.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 30:16 — *And Jacob came out of the field in the evening, and Leah went out to meet him, and said, Thou must come in unto me; for surely I have hired thee with my son’s mandrakes. And he lay with her that night.* *Nevertheless, for the mandrakes I am hiring Jacob to thee for one night.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 62:14) Both tellings call the night a purchase: Genesis has Leah say she had hired Jacob with her son's mandrakes, and the Testament of Issachar has Rachel say "I am hiring Jacob to thee for one night." The Testament only changes who is speaking.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',62,12,$n$For thou art not his wife, but in craft wert taken to him in my stead.$n$,'canon','genesis',29,25,$n$And it came to pass, that in the morning, behold, it was Leah: and he said to Laban, What is this thou hast done unto me? did not I serve with thee for Rachel? wherefore then hast thou beguiled me?$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 29:25 — *And it came to pass, that in the morning, behold, it was Leah: and he said to Laban, What is this thou hast done unto me? did not I serve with thee for Rachel? wherefore then hast thou beguiled me?* *For thou art not his wife, but in craft wert taken to him in my stead.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 62:12) Rachel's complaint in the Testament of Issachar, that Leah was taken to Jacob "in craft," is the very thing Jacob says to Laban the morning after: "wherefore then hast thou beguiled me?" Laban's trick lies underneath the whole quarrel over the mandrakes.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',63,4,$n$For on the morrow also she again gave up Jacob. Because of the mandrakes, therefore, Yahuah (Lord) hearkened to Rachel.$n$,'canon','genesis',30,22,$n$And Elohim (God) remembered Rachel, and Elohim (God) hearkened to her, and opened her womb.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 30:22 — *And Elohim (God) remembered Rachel, and Elohim (God) hearkened to her, and opened her womb.* *For on the morrow also she again gave up Jacob. Because of the mandrakes, therefore, Yahuah (Lord) hearkened to Rachel.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 63:4) Genesis says Elohim (God) remembered Rachel and hearkened to her, and the Testament of Issachar says the very same of Yahuah (the Lord): He hearkened to Rachel. The mandrakes are not the point; the hearing is.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',63,3,$n$For He knew that for the sake of children she wished to company with Jacob, and not for lust of pleasure.$n$,'canon','genesis',30,23,$n$And she conceived, and bare a son; and said, Elohim (God) hath taken away my reproach:$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 30:23 — *And she conceived, and bare a son; and said, Elohim (God) hath taken away my reproach:* *For He knew that for the sake of children she wished to company with Jacob, and not for lust of pleasure.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 63:3) The Testament of Issachar says Rachel wished for children and "not for lust of pleasure," and Genesis shows what she was really carrying: the reproach of barrenness, which Elohim (God) took away when Joseph was born.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',63,1,$n$Then appeared to Jacob an angel of Yahuah (Lord), saying: Two children shall Rachel bear, inasmuch as she hath refused company with her husband, and hath chosen continency.$n$,'canon','genesis',30,24,$n$And she called his name Joseph; and said, Yahuah (LORD) shall add to me another son.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 30:24 — *And she called his name Joseph; and said, Yahuah (LORD) shall add to me another son.* *Then appeared to Jacob an angel of Yahuah (Lord), saying: Two children shall Rachel bear, inasmuch as she hath refused company with her husband, and hath chosen continency.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 63:1) When the Testament of Issachar has an angel tell Jacob that Rachel shall bear "Two children," it is looking at the same hope Rachel voices at Joseph's birth, a second son to follow the first. Genesis does not mention the angel, so the reader should set the angel's word beside Rachel's and let Genesis stand as the record.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',63,5,$n$For though she desired them, she eat them not, but offered them in the house of Yahuah (Lord), presenting them to the priest of the El Elyon (Most High) who was at that time.$n$,'canon','genesis',14,18,$n$And Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek) king of Salem brought forth bread and wine: and he was the priest of the El Elyon (most high God).$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 14:18 — *And Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek) king of Salem brought forth bread and wine: and he was the priest of the El Elyon (most high God).* *For though she desired them, she eat them not, but offered them in the house of Yahuah (Lord), presenting them to the priest of the El Elyon (Most High) who was at that time.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 63:5) The Testament of Issachar says Rachel offered the mandrakes in the house of Yahuah (the Lord) to the priest of the El Elyon (Most High) of that time. Genesis does tell of Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek) as priest of the El Elyon (most high God) in the days of Abraham, but nowhere says that mandrakes were brought to any priest, so the reader has Genesis for the priest and the Testament for the offering and no more than that.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',81,1,$n$For anger is an evil thing, my children, for it troubleth even the soul itself.$n$,'canon','proverbs',14,29,$n$He that is slow to wrath is of great understanding: but he that is hasty of spirit exalteth folly.$n$,'free',$n$Proverbs 14:29 — *He that is slow to wrath is of great understanding: but he that is hasty of spirit exalteth folly.* *For anger is an evil thing, my children, for it troubleth even the soul itself.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 81:1) The Testament of Dan says anger "troubleth even the soul itself," and Proverbs says the same from the other side: the man quick to wrath is the one who lifts folly high. Both begin with what anger does to the man who carries it.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',81,3,$n$And when the body does all these things, the soul justifieth what is done, since it seeth not aright.$n$,'canon','james',1,20,$n$For the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of Elohim (God).$n$,'free',$n$James 1:20 — *For the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of Elohim (God).* *And when the body does all these things, the soul justifieth what is done, since it seeth not aright.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 81:3) The Testament of Dan says the soul of an angry man "justifieth what is done, since it seeth not aright." James answers that wrath cannot produce the righteousness Elohim (God) asks for, however right it feels to the one who is angry.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',81,5,$n$And though the wrathful man be weak, yet hath he a power twofold of that which is by nature; for wrath ever aideth such in lawlessness.$n$,'canon','proverbs',29,22,$n$An angry man stirreth up strife, and a furious man aboundeth in transgression.$n$,'free',$n$Proverbs 29:22 — *An angry man stirreth up strife, and a furious man aboundeth in transgression.* *And though the wrathful man be weak, yet hath he a power twofold of that which is by nature; for wrath ever aideth such in lawlessness.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 81:5) The Testament of Dan says wrath "ever aideth such in lawlessness," and Proverbs says that a furious man aboundeth in transgression. The two lines are one observation: anger multiplies sin.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',81,6,$n$This spirit goeth always with lying 'at the right hand of Satan, that with cruelty and lying' his works may be wrought.$n$,'canon','ephesians',4,27,$n$Neither give place to the devil.$n$,'free',$n$Ephesians 4:27 — *Neither give place to the devil.* *This spirit goeth always with lying 'at the right hand of Satan, that with cruelty and lying' his works may be wrought.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 81:6) The Testament of Dan joins anger to lying and puts both at the right hand of Satan. The short line in Ephesians gives the same warning in a few words, that anger can open a door.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',85,3,$n$[Nevertheless, 'Dan' prophesied unto them that they should forget their Elohim (God), and should be alienated from the land of their inheritance 'and from the race of Israel', and from the family of 'their seed'.]$n$,'canon','deuteronomy',32,18,$n$Of the Rock that begat thee thou art unmindful, and hast forgotten Elohim (God) that formed thee.$n$,'free',$n$Deuteronomy 32:18 — *Of the Rock that begat thee thou art unmindful, and hast forgotten Elohim (God) that formed thee.* *[Nevertheless, 'Dan' prophesied unto them that they should forget their Elohim (God), and should be alienated from the land of their inheritance 'and from the race of Israel', and from the family of 'their seed'.]* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 85:3) The Testament of Dan says his sons "should forget their Elohim (God)," and Moses had already put that charge in a song: Yashar'el (Israel) forgot Elohim (God) who formed them. The bracketed sentence is Dan's sorrow, and the song is Moses' warning.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',85,3,$n$[Nevertheless, 'Dan' prophesied unto them that they should forget their Elohim (God), and should be alienated from the land of their inheritance 'and from the race of Israel', and from the family of 'their seed'.]$n$,'canon','judges',18,30,$n$And the children of Dan set up the graven image: and Jonathan, the son of Gershom, the son of Manasseh, he and his sons were priests to the tribe of Dan until the day of the captivity of the land.$n$,'free',$n$Judges 18:30 — *And the children of Dan set up the graven image: and Jonathan, the son of Gershom, the son of Manasseh, he and his sons were priests to the tribe of Dan until the day of the captivity of the land.* *[Nevertheless, 'Dan' prophesied unto them that they should forget their Elohim (God), and should be alienated from the land of their inheritance 'and from the race of Israel', and from the family of 'their seed'.]* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 85:3) Scripture records how the tribe of Dan set up a graven image for itself and kept priests for it until the day of the captivity of the land. This is the scripture beside the Testament of Dan's saying that his sons would be "alienated from the land of their inheritance." The sentence in the Testament is bracketed in the translation, so the reader should let Judges stand as the witness.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',85,2,$n$And his sons buried him. And after that they carried up 'his bones', and placed them near Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob.$n$,'canon','genesis',49,31,$n$There they buried Abraham and Sarah his wife; there they buried Isaac and Rebekah his wife; and there I buried Leah.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 49:31 — *There they buried Abraham and Sarah his wife; there they buried Isaac and Rebekah his wife; and there I buried Leah.* *And his sons buried him. And after that they carried up 'his bones', and placed them near Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 85:2) The Testament of Dan says his sons carried up his bones and "placed them near Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob." Genesis names the cave where Abraham and Sarah, Isaac and Rebekah, and Leah were buried, which is the burying place the fathers' sons were laid beside. It does not say where Dan was buried, so the Testament speaks for itself there.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',92,2,$n$Then my father saith unto me: I believe Elohim (God) that Joseph liveth, for I see 'always' that Yahuah (Lord) numbereth him with you.$n$,'canon','genesis',45,28,$n$And Yashar'el (Israel) said, It is enough; Joseph my son is yet alive: I will go and see him before I die.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 45:28 — *And Yashar'el (Israel) said, It is enough; Joseph my son is yet alive: I will go and see him before I die.* *Then my father saith unto me: I believe Elohim (God) that Joseph liveth, for I see 'always' that Yahuah (Lord) numbereth him with you.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 92:2) In the Testament of Naphtali, Jacob says he believes Elohim (God) that Joseph lives, and when the news finally comes in Genesis the old man says it plainly: "Joseph my son is yet alive." What he held in hope the wagons made certain.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',92,1,$n$These two dreams I told to my father; and he said to me: These things must be fulfilled in their season, after that Yashar'el (Israel) hath endured many things.$n$,'canon','genesis',37,11,$n$And his brethren envied him; but his father observed the saying.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 37:11 — *And his brethren envied him; but his father observed the saying.* *These two dreams I told to my father; and he said to me: These things must be fulfilled in their season, after that Yashar'el (Israel) hath endured many things.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 92:1) Joseph's father in Genesis "observed the saying" of the dreams, and in the Testament of Naphtali he tells his son the dreams must be fulfilled in their season, after Yashar'el (Israel) has endured many things. Both show a father who kept the dreams in mind.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',92,3,$n$And he said, weeping: Ah me, my son, Joseph, thou livest, though I behold thee not, and thou seest not Jacob that begat thee.$n$,'canon','genesis',37,35,$n$And all his sons and all his daughters rose up to comfort him; but he refused to be comforted; and he said, For I will go down into the grave unto my son mourning. Thus his father wept for him.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 37:35 — *And all his sons and all his daughters rose up to comfort him; but he refused to be comforted; and he said, For I will go down into the grave unto my son mourning. Thus his father wept for him.* *And he said, weeping: Ah me, my son, Joseph, thou livest, though I behold thee not, and thou seest not Jacob that begat thee.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 92:3) Genesis tells that Jacob refused comfort and said he would go down mourning to the grave, and the Testament of Naphtali has him weeping, "Ah me, my son, Joseph, thou livest, though I behold thee not." The tears are the same; the Testament adds that his hope was alive under them.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',92,4,$n$He caused me also, therefore, to weep by these words, and I burned in my heart to declare that 'Joseph' had been sold, but I feared my brethren.$n$,'canon','genesis',37,33,$n$And he knew it, and said, It is my son’s coat; an evil beast hath devoured him; Joseph is without doubt rent in pieces.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 37:33 — *And he knew it, and said, It is my son’s coat; an evil beast hath devoured him; Joseph is without doubt rent in pieces.* *He caused me also, therefore, to weep by these words, and I burned in my heart to declare that 'Joseph' had been sold, but I feared my brethren.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 92:4) Naphtali confesses he burned to declare that Joseph had been sold, but feared his brethren. Genesis shows what the silence cost, for Jacob looked at the coat and cried that Joseph was without doubt rent in pieces.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',94,1,$n$And when he had charged them with many such words, he exhorted them that they should remove his bones to Hebron, and that they should bury him with his fathers.$n$,'canon','genesis',49,29,$n$And he charged them, and said unto them, I am to be gathered unto my people: bury me with my fathers in the cave that is in the field of Ephron the Hittite,$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 49:29 — *And he charged them, and said unto them, I am to be gathered unto my people: bury me with my fathers in the cave that is in the field of Ephron the Hittite,* *And when he had charged them with many such words, he exhorted them that they should remove his bones to Hebron, and that they should bury him with his fathers.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 94:1) Naphtali charges his sons to bury him with his fathers, as Jacob had charged his own. The Testament of Naphtali and Genesis speak with one voice at the deathbed: a patriarch who wishes to lie with his people.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',94,1,$n$And when he had charged them with many such words, he exhorted them that they should remove his bones to Hebron, and that they should bury him with his fathers.$n$,'canon','genesis',23,19,$n$And after this, Abraham buried Sarah his wife in the cave of the field of Machpelah before Mamre: the same is Hebron in the land of Canaan.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 23:19 — *And after this, Abraham buried Sarah his wife in the cave of the field of Machpelah before Mamre: the same is Hebron in the land of Canaan.* *And when he had charged them with many such words, he exhorted them that they should remove his bones to Hebron, and that they should bury him with his fathers.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 94:1) The Testament of Naphtali names Hebron as the place for his bones, and Genesis is where Hebron first becomes a burying place, when Abraham buried Sarah there. The fathers' graves are the reason Naphtali asks to be taken there.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',94,2,$n$And when he had eaten and drunken with a merry heart, he covered his face and died.$n$,'canon','genesis',49,33,$n$And when Jacob had made an end of commanding his sons, he gathered up his feet into the bed, and yielded up the ghost, and was gathered unto his people.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 49:33 — *And when Jacob had made an end of commanding his sons, he gathered up his feet into the bed, and yielded up the ghost, and was gathered unto his people.* *And when he had eaten and drunken with a merry heart, he covered his face and died.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 94:2) The Testament of Naphtali says he "covered his face and died," and Genesis closes Jacob's life with the same plainness: he gathered up his feet and was gathered to his people. In both, a father finishes his commands and then departs.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',94,3,$n$And his sons did according to all that Naphtali their father had commanded them.$n$,'canon','genesis',50,12,$n$And his sons did unto him according as he commanded them:$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 50:12 — *And his sons did unto him according as he commanded them:* *And his sons did according to all that Naphtali their father had commanded them.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 94:3) The Testament of Naphtali says his sons "did according to all that Naphtali their father had commanded them." Genesis has the very same obedience from Jacob's sons when they buried their father.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',110,1,$n$And when he had said these things unto them, he commanded saying: Bury me in Hebron. And he fell asleep and died at a good old age.$n$,'canon','genesis',25,8,$n$Then Abraham gave up the ghost, and died in a good old age, an old man, and full of years; and was gathered to his people.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 25:8 — *Then Abraham gave up the ghost, and died in a good old age, an old man, and full of years; and was gathered to his people.* *And when he had said these things unto them, he commanded saying: Bury me in Hebron. And he fell asleep and died at a good old age.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 110:1) Asher falls asleep "at a good old age," the same words Genesis uses of Abraham. Both end full of days and gathered to their people.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',110,1,$n$And when he had said these things unto them, he commanded saying: Bury me in Hebron. And he fell asleep and died at a good old age.$n$,'canon','genesis',49,31,$n$There they buried Abraham and Sarah his wife; there they buried Isaac and Rebekah his wife; and there I buried Leah.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 49:31 — *There they buried Abraham and Sarah his wife; there they buried Isaac and Rebekah his wife; and there I buried Leah.* *And when he had said these things unto them, he commanded saying: Bury me in Hebron. And he fell asleep and died at a good old age.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 110:1) Asher's command, "Bury me in Hebron," asks for the place where Genesis says Abraham and Sarah lay, and Isaac and Rebekah, and Leah. The fathers' burying place is what his request is aimed at.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',110,2,$n$And his sons did as he had commanded them, and they carried him up to Hebron, and buried him with his fathers.$n$,'canon','genesis',50,13,$n$For his sons carried him into the land of Canaan, and buried him in the cave of the field of Machpelah, which Abraham bought with the field for a possession of a buryingplace of Ephron the Hittite, before Mamre.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 50:13 — *For his sons carried him into the land of Canaan, and buried him in the cave of the field of Machpelah, which Abraham bought with the field for a possession of a buryingplace of Ephron the Hittite, before Mamre.* *And his sons did as he had commanded them, and they carried him up to Hebron, and buried him with his fathers.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 110:2) The sons of Asher "carried him up to Hebron, and buried him with his fathers," and Genesis tells how Jacob's sons carried their father to the same cave of Machpelah. The Testament of Asher closes with the same obedience.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',115,2,$n$I therefore, when I heard this, rent my garments, and said unto her: Woman, reverence Elohim (God), and do not this evil deed, lest thou be destroyed; for know indeed that I will declare this thy device unto all men.$n$,'canon','genesis',39,9,$n$There is none greater in this house than I; neither hath he kept back any thing from me but thee, because thou art his wife: how then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against Elohim (God)?$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 39:9 — *There is none greater in this house than I; neither hath he kept back any thing from me but thee, because thou art his wife: how then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against Elohim (God)?* *I therefore, when I heard this, rent my garments, and said unto her: Woman, reverence Elohim (God), and do not this evil deed, lest thou be destroyed; for know indeed that I will declare this thy device unto all men.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 115:2) Joseph in the Testament of Joseph tells the woman to "reverence Elohim (God)" and refuse this evil deed, and in Genesis his refusal turns on the same fear: how can he do this great wickedness and sin against Elohim (God)? The sin is first against Elohim (God), and only then against the master.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',115,1,$n$'And' again, at another time she said unto me: If thou wilt not commit adultery, I will kill my husband by poison, and take thee to be my husband.$n$,'canon','exodus',20,14,$n$Thou shalt not commit adultery.$n$,'free',$n$Exodus 20:14 — *Thou shalt not commit adultery.* *'And' again, at another time she said unto me: If thou wilt not commit adultery, I will kill my husband by poison, and take thee to be my husband.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 115:1) The woman's threat to kill her husband by poison so that she may take Joseph is a plot against this commandment from the start, and Joseph's answer in the Testament of Joseph is to refuse adultery. The commandment is the ground he stands on.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',115,1,$n$'And' again, at another time she said unto me: If thou wilt not commit adultery, I will kill my husband by poison, and take thee to be my husband.$n$,'canon','proverbs',6,26,$n$For by means of a whorish woman a man is brought to a piece of bread: and the adulteress will hunt for the precious life.$n$,'free',$n$Proverbs 6:26 — *For by means of a whorish woman a man is brought to a piece of bread: and the adulteress will hunt for the precious life.* *'And' again, at another time she said unto me: If thou wilt not commit adultery, I will kill my husband by poison, and take thee to be my husband.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 115:1) The Testament of Joseph has the woman offer to poison her husband, which shows how the adulteress "will hunt for the precious life" in Proverbs. The proverb warns a young man of exactly this woman.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',115,4,$n$And she departed, soothing me with gifts, and sending to me every delight of the sons of men.$n$,'canon','proverbs',7,21,$n$With her much fair speech she caused him to yield, with the flattering of her lips she forced him.$n$,'free',$n$Proverbs 7:21 — *With her much fair speech she caused him to yield, with the flattering of her lips she forced him.* *And she departed, soothing me with gifts, and sending to me every delight of the sons of men.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 115:4) When Joseph refuses, she goes away "soothing me with gifts," and the Proverbs describe how fair speech and flattering lips have forced the simple to yield. The gifts are the same snare as the fair words.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',117,3,$n$Then accordingly seizing an opportunity she rushed unto me while her husband was yet without, and said unto me: I will hang myself, or cast myself over a cliff, if thou wilt not lie with me.$n$,'canon','genesis',39,10,$n$And it came to pass, as she spake to Joseph day by day, that he hearkened not unto her, to lie by her, or to be with her.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 39:10 — *And it came to pass, as she spake to Joseph day by day, that he hearkened not unto her, to lie by her, or to be with her.* *Then accordingly seizing an opportunity she rushed unto me while her husband was yet without, and said unto me: I will hang myself, or cast myself over a cliff, if thou wilt not lie with me.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 117:3) Genesis says the woman spoke to Joseph "day by day," and the Testament of Joseph shows what that daily pressure looked like when it grew desperate, with threats to hang herself or cast herself over a cliff. In both, Joseph does not hearken.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',117,7,$n$But she knew not that because of my lord I spake thus, and not because of her.$n$,'canon','genesis',39,8,$n$But he refused, and said unto his master’s wife, Behold, my master wotteth not what is with me in the house, and he hath committed all that he hath to my hand;$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 39:8 — *But he refused, and said unto his master’s wife, Behold, my master wotteth not what is with me in the house, and he hath committed all that he hath to my hand;* *But she knew not that because of my lord I spake thus, and not because of her.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 117:7) The Testament of Joseph says "because of my lord I spake thus, and not because of her," and Genesis gives Joseph's plea to the woman in the same spirit: his master had trusted him with all that he had. His loyalty was to the man who trusted him.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',117,8,$n$For if a man hath fallen before the passion of a wicked desire and become enslaved by it, even as she, whatever good thing he may hear with regard to that passion, he receiveth it with a view to his wicked desire.$n$,'canon','proverbs',5,22,$n$His own iniquities shall take the wicked himself, and he shall be holden with the cords of his sins.$n$,'free',$n$Proverbs 5:22 — *His own iniquities shall take the wicked himself, and he shall be holden with the cords of his sins.* *For if a man hath fallen before the passion of a wicked desire and become enslaved by it, even as she, whatever good thing he may hear with regard to that passion, he receiveth it with a view to his wicked desire.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 117:8) The Testament of Joseph says that a man who has "become enslaved" to a wicked desire hears every good word as a help to that desire. Proverbs says the same of the wicked: his own iniquities take him and he is held with the cords of his sins.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',122,3,$n$Now, therefore, render justice unto him, and take away the youth to thy house; so shall the Elohim (God) of the Hebrews bless thee, for grace from heaven is upon him.$n$,'canon','genesis',39,3,$n$And his master saw that Yahuah (LORD) was with him, and that Yahuah (LORD) made all that he did to prosper in his hand.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 39:3 — *And his master saw that Yahuah (LORD) was with him, and that Yahuah (LORD) made all that he did to prosper in his hand.* *Now, therefore, render justice unto him, and take away the youth to thy house; so shall the Elohim (God) of the Hebrews bless thee, for grace from heaven is upon him.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 122:3) The woman in the Testament of Joseph tells her husband that "grace from heaven is upon" the young Hebrew. Genesis says the same thing about what Potiphar himself saw: that Yahuah (the LORD) was with Joseph and made all he did to prosper.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',122,3,$n$Now, therefore, render justice unto him, and take away the youth to thy house; so shall the Elohim (God) of the Hebrews bless thee, for grace from heaven is upon him.$n$,'canon','genesis',39,5,$n$And it came to pass from the time that he had made him overseer in his house, and over all that he had, that Yahuah (LORD) blessed the Egyptian’s house for Joseph’s sake; and the blessing of Yahuah (LORD) was upon all that he had in the house, and in the field.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 39:5 — *And it came to pass from the time that he had made him overseer in his house, and over all that he had, that Yahuah (LORD) blessed the Egyptian’s house for Joseph’s sake; and the blessing of Yahuah (LORD) was upon all that he had in the house, and in the field.* *Now, therefore, render justice unto him, and take away the youth to thy house; so shall the Elohim (God) of the Hebrews bless thee, for grace from heaven is upon him.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 122:3) The Testament of Joseph has the woman say that the Elohim (God) of the Hebrews would bless her husband for the youth's sake, and Genesis tells that Yahuah (the LORD) blessed the Egyptian's house for Joseph's sake. The blessing came as she said, whatever else she was saying it for.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',122,2,$n$And she told her husband that the merchant had become rich by means of a young Hebrew, and they say that he had assuredly been stolen out of the land of Canaan.$n$,'canon','genesis',40,15,$n$For indeed I was stolen away out of the land of the Hebrews: and here also have I done nothing that they should put me into the dungeon.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 40:15 — *For indeed I was stolen away out of the land of the Hebrews: and here also have I done nothing that they should put me into the dungeon.* *And she told her husband that the merchant had become rich by means of a young Hebrew, and they say that he had assuredly been stolen out of the land of Canaan.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 122:2) Joseph in Genesis says he was "stolen away out of the land of the Hebrews," and the Egyptian woman in the Testament of Joseph says that he was stolen out of the land of Canaan. In both the claim is that this was a captive and not a slave by right.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',122,1,$n$And about that time the Memphian woman, the wife of Pentephri, came down 'in a chariot', 'with great pomp, because she had heard from her eunuchs concerning me'.$n$,'canon','genesis',39,1,$n$And Joseph was brought down to Egypt; and Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh, captain of the guard, an Egyptian, bought him of the hands of the Ishmeelites, which had brought him down thither.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 39:1 — *And Joseph was brought down to Egypt; and Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh, captain of the guard, an Egyptian, bought him of the hands of the Ishmeelites, which had brought him down thither.* *And about that time the Memphian woman, the wife of Pentephri, came down 'in a chariot', 'with great pomp, because she had heard from her eunuchs concerning me'.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 122:1) Genesis tells simply that Potiphar, the captain of the guard, bought Joseph from the Ishmeelites who brought him down. The Testament of Joseph tells of a merchant, a chariot and the woman's eunuchs, which are details of its own, while the buyer and the seller in Genesis stand as the record.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',123,8,$n$And he said: How didst thou become their slave? And I said: They bought me out of the land of Canaan.$n$,'canon','genesis',37,28,$n$Then there passed by Midianites merchantmen; and they drew and lifted up Joseph out of the pit, and sold Joseph to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver: and they brought Joseph into Egypt.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 37:28 — *Then there passed by Midianites merchantmen; and they drew and lifted up Joseph out of the pit, and sold Joseph to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver: and they brought Joseph into Egypt.* *And he said: How didst thou become their slave? And I said: They bought me out of the land of Canaan.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 123:8) When Joseph says in the Testament of Joseph that "They bought me out of the land of Canaan," he is telling in short what Genesis tells: he was sold to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver and brought into Egypt.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',123,3,$n$And Pentephris said unto him: Whence, then, is the Hebrew slave? And he said: The Ishmaelites entrusted him to me until they should return.$n$,'canon','genesis',39,1,$n$And Joseph was brought down to Egypt; and Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh, captain of the guard, an Egyptian, bought him of the hands of the Ishmeelites, which had brought him down thither.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 39:1 — *And Joseph was brought down to Egypt; and Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh, captain of the guard, an Egyptian, bought him of the hands of the Ishmeelites, which had brought him down thither.* *And Pentephris said unto him: Whence, then, is the Hebrew slave? And he said: The Ishmaelites entrusted him to me until they should return.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 123:3) The merchant in the Testament of Joseph says "The Ishmaelites entrusted him to me." Genesis names the Ishmeelites too, as those who brought Joseph down to Egypt and sold him to Potiphar.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',123,6,$n$And he took me apart from him, and said unto me: Art thou a slave or free? And I said: A slave.$n$,'canon','psalms',105,17,$n$He sent a man before them, even Joseph, who was sold for a servant:$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 105:17 — *He sent a man before them, even Joseph, who was sold for a servant:* *And he took me apart from him, and said unto me: Art thou a slave or free? And I said: A slave.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 123:6) When Pentephris asks "Art thou a slave or free?" and Joseph answers "A slave," he is saying what the psalm says: he was sold for a servant. The psalm adds that he was a man sent before them, so the selling was also a sending.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',123,9,$n$And he said unto me: 'Truly' thou liest; and 'straightway' he commanded me to be stripped and beaten.$n$,'canon','psalms',105,18,$n$Whose feet they hurt with fetters: he was laid in iron:$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 105:18 — *Whose feet they hurt with fetters: he was laid in iron:* *And he said unto me: 'Truly' thou liest; and 'straightway' he commanded me to be stripped and beaten.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 123:9) The Testament of Joseph tells that Joseph was stripped and beaten for his answer, and the psalm says that his feet were hurt with fetters and he was laid in iron. The suffering is told differently, but both put it on a man who had done nothing.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',124,2,$n$And when I made no change in my statement, 'though I was beaten', he ordered me to be imprisoned, until, he said, the owners of the boy should come.$n$,'canon','genesis',39,20,$n$And Joseph’s master took him, and put him into the prison, a place where the king’s prisoners were bound: and he was there in the prison.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 39:20 — *And Joseph’s master took him, and put him into the prison, a place where the king’s prisoners were bound: and he was there in the prison.* *And when I made no change in my statement, 'though I was beaten', he ordered me to be imprisoned, until, he said, the owners of the boy should come.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 124:2) Joseph in the Testament of Joseph is imprisoned "the owners of the boy should come," and Genesis tells where he was put: the prison, a place where the king's prisoners were bound. The reason the two give is different, but the prison is the same.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',124,4,$n$For she wished to see me out of a desire 'of sin', but I was ignorant concerning all these things.$n$,'canon','genesis',39,7,$n$And it came to pass after these things, that his master’s wife cast her eyes upon Joseph; and she said, Lie with me.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 39:7 — *And it came to pass after these things, that his master’s wife cast her eyes upon Joseph; and she said, Lie with me.* *For she wished to see me out of a desire 'of sin', but I was ignorant concerning all these things.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 124:4) The Testament of Joseph says the woman wished to see him "out of a desire" while Joseph "was ignorant concerning all these things". Genesis gives the same fact in a few words: she cast her eyes upon Joseph and said, Lie with me.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',124,2,$n$And when I made no change in my statement, 'though I was beaten', he ordered me to be imprisoned, until, he said, the owners of the boy should come.$n$,'canon','psalms',105,19,$n$Until the time that his word came: the word of Yahuah (LORD) tried him.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 105:19 — *Until the time that his word came: the word of Yahuah (LORD) tried him.* *And when I made no change in my statement, 'though I was beaten', he ordered me to be imprisoned, until, he said, the owners of the boy should come.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 124:2) In the Testament of Joseph, Joseph sticks to his story though beaten and is imprisoned. The psalm says what the prison was for: until the time that his word came, the word of Yahuah (the LORD) tried him.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',126,6,$n$And though I knew (this) I held my peace, lest the eunuch should be put to shame.$n$,'canon','proverbs',12,16,$n$A fool’s wrath is presently known: but a prudent man covereth shame.$n$,'free',$n$Proverbs 12:16 — *A fool’s wrath is presently known: but a prudent man covereth shame.* *And though I knew (this) I held my peace, lest the eunuch should be put to shame.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 126:6) Joseph in the Testament of Joseph knew the eunuch had cheated him on the price, and "held my peace, lest the eunuch should be put to shame." Proverbs calls that kind of silence prudence, a prudent man covers shame while a fool's wrath is known at once.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',126,2,$n$And 'straightway' she sent a eunuch to the Ishmaelites, and asked them to sell me.$n$,'canon','genesis',37,36,$n$And the Midianites sold him into Egypt unto Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh’s, and captain of the guard.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 37:36 — *And the Midianites sold him into Egypt unto Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh’s, and captain of the guard.* *And 'straightway' she sent a eunuch to the Ishmaelites, and asked them to sell me.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 126:2) The Testament of Joseph has the woman send a eunuch to the Ishmaelites to buy Joseph. Genesis closes the chapter of his sale with Joseph already sold into Egypt to Potiphar, the captain of the guard.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','testaments-xii',126,5,$n$The eunuch therefore went and gave them eighty pieces of gold, and 'he received me'; but to the Egyptian woman he said: I have given a hundred.$n$,'canon','genesis',37,28,$n$Then there passed by Midianites merchantmen; and they drew and lifted up Joseph out of the pit, and sold Joseph to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver: and they brought Joseph into Egypt.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 37:28 — *Then there passed by Midianites merchantmen; and they drew and lifted up Joseph out of the pit, and sold Joseph to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver: and they brought Joseph into Egypt.* *The eunuch therefore went and gave them eighty pieces of gold, and 'he received me'; but to the Egyptian woman he said: I have given a hundred.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 126:5) The eunuch in the Testament of Joseph "gave them eighty pieces of gold" and told his mistress it was a hundred. Genesis says the first price, when Joseph was sold to the Ishmeelites, was twenty pieces of silver. The sums differ, and the Testament should be read as a separate telling.$n$);
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s495_testaments_xii_t (slug TEXT, title TEXT, summary_md TEXT, ch INT, a_vn INT, z_vn INT, tier TEXT, sort_order INT) ON COMMIT DROP;
INSERT INTO _s495_testaments_xii_t VALUES
('testaments-xii-2-the-eye-desire-and-the-pit',$n$The eye, desire, and the pit that youth walks into$n$,$n$It ain't new. When Reuben says the sense of sight is where desire arises, and that the last of the seven spirits leads a young man like a blind man to a pit, he is saying what Genesis showed in the first garden and what Proverbs showed in the street. The Testament sorts the body into spirits and senses in its own way, and that sorting is not what the reader is asked to carry away; the warning is.

*The first is the spirit of life, with which the constitution (of man) is created. The second is the sense of sight, with which ariseth desire.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 2:4)

Moses had already told the first of it. The woman looked before she took, and the whole road from the eye to the hand is laid out in one verse.

*And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat.* (Genesis 3:6)

Then the Testament speaks of what that desire does to the young.

*Wherefore it is the last in order of creation, and the first in that of youth, because it is filled with ignorance, and leadeth the youth as a blind man to a pit, and as a beast to a precipice.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 2:9)

Solomon's counsel in Proverbs draws that picture one street at a time, a young man who goes after her straightway and does not know where the road ends until it is upon him.

*He goeth after her straightway, as an ox goeth to the slaughter, or as a fool to the correction of the stocks;* (Proverbs 7:22)

Job gives the answer to both, and it is short: a covenant made with the eyes before the sight ever arrives. Reuben, speaking out of his own repentance, is pointing his sons the same way.

*I made a covenant with mine eyes; why then should I think upon a maid?* (Job 31:1)$n$,2,4,9,'extras',94000),
('testaments-xii-7-reuben-in-a-coffin-until-carried-up',$n$Reuben laid in a coffin until he could be carried up$n$,$n$Reuben dies after giving his commands, and his sons do what the sons of Jacob had done before them: they place him in a coffin in Egypt and wait for the day he can be carried up to the land.

*And they placed him in a coffin until they carried him up from Egypt, and buried him in Hebron in the cave where his father was.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 7:2)

Genesis tells how that waiting began, with Joseph, who made the sons of Yashar'el (Israel) swear that his bones would go up with them when Elohim (God) visited them. A coffin in Egypt was never meant to be the end of the story; it was a promise kept in a box until the day of the visiting.

*So Joseph died, being an hundred and ten years old: and they embalmed him, and he was put in a coffin in Egypt.* (Genesis 50:26)

The Testament says Reuben was laid in the cave where his father was, and Genesis names that cave, the field of Machpelah, bought by Abraham, where Jacob's sons buried their father.

*For his sons carried him into the land of Canaan, and buried him in the cave of the field of Machpelah, which Abraham bought with the field for a possession of a buryingplace of Ephron the Hittite, before Mamre.* (Genesis 50:13)

Genesis speaks of Jacob there and says nothing of where each of the other sons was buried, so the Testament's report about Reuben is held as its own word and nothing here is built on it. What the scripture does give is the posture: the sons of Yashar'el (Israel) in Egypt buried their dead with their eyes on the land that Yahuah (the LORD) had sworn to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.$n$,7,1,2,'extras',94005),
('testaments-xii-8-simeon-sits-up-to-speak',$n$Simeon sits up to speak to his sons$n$,$n$It ain't new. The sons come to visit a sick father, and the father strengthens himself and sits up to speak, and Genesis had already drawn that deathbed. Simeon's chapter opens in the same room Jacob once lay in.

*For when Simeon was sick, his sons came to visit him, and he strengthened himself and sat up and kissed them, and said:—* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 8:2)

When Joseph came to Jacob in his sickness, the old man gathered what strength he had so that his last words could be heard, and the Testament shows Simeon doing the very thing.

*And one told Jacob, and said, Behold, thy son Joseph cometh unto thee: and Yashar'el (Israel) strengthened himself, and sat upon the bed.* (Genesis 48:2)

Simeon's own ending is dated by another death, the year his brother Joseph died, and Genesis closes Joseph's life with the same grave and the coffin in Egypt.

*The copy of the words of Simeon, the things which he spake to his sons before he died, in the hundred and twentieth year of his life, at which time Joseph, his brother, died.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 8:1)

*So Joseph died, being an hundred and ten years old: and they embalmed him, and he was put in a coffin in Egypt.* (Genesis 50:26)

That is why the words Simeon is about to speak carry the weight they do. He is speaking in the shadow of the brother he once could not bear to have near him, now laid to rest, and he wants his sons to learn from what he did.$n$,8,1,2,'extras',94010),
('testaments-xii-16-in-egypt-until-the-hand-of-moses',$n$Simeon's sons in Egypt until the hand of Moses$n$,$n$It ain't new. The Testament of Simeon ends with the sons mourning their father and then waiting in Egypt until the day of departure by the hand of Moses, and that is the exodus as Exodus and the Psalms tell it. The Testament needs only to point, because the reader already knows what the hand of Moses did.

*And they were in Egypt until the day of their departure by the hand of Moses.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 16:2)

Exodus gives the day the waiting ended. The four hundred and thirty years had a last day, and the hosts of Yahuah (the LORD) went out on it.

*And it came to pass at the end of the four hundred and thirty years, even the selfsame day it came to pass, that all the hosts of Yahuah (LORD) went out from the land of Egypt.* (Exodus 12:41)

The psalmist remembers the leaving as a shepherd's work, Yahuah (the LORD) leading his people like a flock, and the staff in the shepherd's hand was Moses.

*Thou leddest thy people like a flock by the hand of Moses and Aaron.* (Psalm 77:20)

The brothers in these Testaments end their lives in Egypt, and their children are still there when the book closes. The promise to carry the bones up, and to bring the people up, was waiting on a day Yahuah (the LORD) had already appointed.$n$,16,1,2,'extras',94015),
('testaments-xii-17-levi-calls-his-sons-before-he-dies',$n$Levi calls his sons because it was revealed he would die$n$,$n$It ain't new. Levi calls his sons together, sound in body, because it had been revealed to him that he would die, and he ordains what they are to do and what will befall them until the day of judgment. A father near the end speaks to his sons about what is coming, and Jacob had done it first.

*The copy of the words of Levi, the things which he ordained unto his sons, according to all that they should do, and what things should befall them until the day of judgment.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 17:1)

Jacob called his sons around him and promised to tell them what would befall them in the last days, and Levi's opening uses the same word for the same kind of telling.

*And Jacob called unto his sons, and said, Gather yourselves together, that I may tell you that which shall befall you in the last days.* (Genesis 49:1)

Levi's second verse says why he spoke while he was well: it had been revealed to him that he should die.

*He was sound in health when he called them to him; for it had been revealed to him that he should die. And when they were gathered together he said to them:* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 17:2)

Peter says almost the same of himself, that he knew his time was short because it had been shown him, and so he spoke. A man who knows the hour does not spend it on small things.

*Knowing that shortly I must put off this my tabernacle, even as our Lord Yahusha HaMashiach (Lord Jesus Christ) hath shewed me.* (2 Peter 1:14)

What Levi tells his sons in the chapters that follow is about their own house and the age to come, and the scripture beside it is the same: what is coming is told to the sons so that they can walk in the commandments and not be surprised.$n$,17,1,2,'extras',94020),
('testaments-xii-23-folly-in-israel-and-jacobs-answer',$n$Folly in Israel, and what Jacob said to Levi$n$,$n$Levi's account of Shechem is told from inside the brothers' anger. Genesis tells the same events, and it keeps the voices Levi's chapter only partly carries: the brothers' grief, the father's rebuke, and the promise that outlived them all.

*Because also they had wrought folly in Yashar'el (Israel) by defiling my sister. And we departed and came to Bethel.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 23:3)

The phrase *folly in Yashar'el (Israel)* is Genesis's own, spoken when the sons of Jacob came in from the field and heard what had been done. The wrong was done to Dinah, and it was done to the whole house.

*And the sons of Jacob came out of the field when they heard it: and the men were grieved, and they were very wroth, because he had wrought folly in Yashar'el (Israel) in lying with Jacob’s daughter; which thing ought not to be done.* (Genesis 34:7)

But the Testament says, a verse earlier, that the brothers mocked the Shechemites, and Genesis does not leave Jacob out of the telling. He told Simeon and Levi they had troubled him, and at the end of his life he spoke of the two sons by name:

*And Jacob said to Simeon and Levi, Ye have troubled me to make me to stink among the inhabitants of the land, among the Canaanites and the Perizzites: and I being few in number, they shall gather themselves together against me, and slay me; and I shall be destroyed, I and my house.* (Genesis 34:30)

*Cursed be their anger, for it was fierce; and their wrath, for it was cruel: I will divide them in Jacob, and scatter them in Yashar'el (Israel).* (Genesis 49:7)

Levi's Testament sits under that word, and nothing in the chapter asks us to set it aside. What the chapter does give is the thing Levi trusted in the first place, that the land was promised to the line.

*And the land which I gave Abraham and Isaac, to thee I will give it, and to thy seed after thee will I give the land.* (Genesis 35:12)$n$,23,1,3,'extras',94025),
('testaments-xii-27-the-sons-of-levi-and-jochebed-born-in-egypt',$n$Levi's sons, and Jochebed born in Egypt$n$,$n$It ain't new. Levi tells over the family that Exodus and Numbers had already counted, and where he adds a year or a vision, scripture is still the ground under it. The births in this chapter are the family history of the priestly line, set down in the order Levi saw them.

*And Jochebed was born in Egypt, in my sixty-fourth year, for I was renowned then in the midst of my brethren.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 27:8)

Numbers puts the same birth in a single line and leaves out the year. Jochebed is born in Egypt to Levi, and she is the woman who bears Aaron and Moses and Miriam.

*And the name of Amram’s wife was Jochebed, the daughter of Levi, whom her mother bare to Levi in Egypt: and she bare unto Amram Aaron and Moses, and Miriam their sister.* (Numbers 26:59)

A name carried the reason a child was born into it. Levi calls his first son Gersam because they were sojourners, and the lists of the sons of Levi spell that name Gershon.

*And she conceived and bare a son, and I called his name Gersam, for we were sojourners in our land.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 27:2)

Moses gave his own son in Midian a name with the same reason in it, a stranger in a land that was not his.

*And she bare him a son, and he called his name Gershom: for he said, I have been a stranger in a strange land.* (Exodus 2:22)

Then Levi looks ahead to Kohath, whom he sees in a vision standing on high in the midst of all the congregation.

*And I saw in a vision that he was standing on high in the midst of all the congregation.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 27:5)

Numbers shows what that standing came to. The sons of Kohath were given the ark and the vessels of the sanctuary to carry, and the vision and the charge fit together.

*And their charge shall be the ark, and the table, and the candlestick, and the altars, and the vessels of the sanctuary wherewith they minister, and the hanging, and all the service thereof.* (Numbers 3:31)$n$,27,2,8,'extras',94030),
('testaments-xii-28-levis-household-and-the-fourth-generation',$n$Levi's household, and the generation that returns$n$,$n$Levi names his grandsons and the marriage of Ambram and Jochebed, then counts his own years and tells his sons that they are a third generation. The counting is not idle, because it follows the line that carries the promise to the day of return.

*And the sons of Kohath, Ambram, Issachar, Hebron, and Ozeel.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 28:2)

These are the sons of Kohath as Exodus gives them, in a slightly different spelling, and the line that comes down from them is the line of Aaron and Moses.

*And the sons of Kohath; Amram, and Izhar, and Hebron, and Uzziel: and the years of the life of Kohath were an hundred thirty and three years.* (Exodus 6:18)

Levi then names the marriage that made that line.

*And in my ninety-fourth year Ambram took Jochebed my daughter to him to wife, for they were born in one day, he and my daughter.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 28:4)

On the thing that matters his account matches Exodus: Amram took Jochebed, and she bare Aaron and Moses. The Testament adds that the two were born in one day, which sits oddly beside Exodus, so this thread rests only on the marriage and its sons.

*And Amram took him Jochebed his father’s sister to wife; and she bare him Aaron and Moses: and the years of the life of Amram were an hundred and thirty and seven years.* (Exodus 6:20)

Then Levi tells his children where they stand in the count.

*And behold, my children, ye are a third generation.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 28:6)

Genesis had given the count before any of these children were born. The sojourning would last until the fourth generation came back, and Levi, numbering his years and his sons, is a man standing inside that count.

*But in the fourth generation they shall come hither again: for the iniquity of the Amorites is not yet full.* (Genesis 15:16)$n$,28,2,7,'extras',94035),
('testaments-xii-31-laid-waste-yet-remembered-for-the-fathers',$n$The temple laid waste, and the seed spared for the fathers' sake$n$,$n$Levi foretells the laying waste of the temple, captivity among all nations, and shame, and then closes on the one reason any of the seed remain at all. All of it was already written in the Torah and the prophets, and none of it puts the blame on the commandments.

*Therefore the temple, which Yahuah (Lord) shall choose, shall be laid waste through your uncleanness, and ye shall be captives throughout all nations.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 31:1)

Ezekiel gives the cause in Yahuah's (the Lord's) own mouth, and it is the defiling of the sanctuary, not the sanctuary and not the instruction that taught Yashar'el (Israel) what was clean.

*Wherefore, as I live, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Surely, because thou hast defiled my sanctuary with all thy detestable things, and with all thine abominations, therefore will I also diminish thee; neither shall mine eye spare, neither will I have any pity.* (Ezekiel 5:11)

The scattering that follows is the exile judgment Moses set out in the curses, a judgment on covenant-breaking, and the Torah that foretold it is the Torah that stands. Then Levi turns, as the prophets always turn, from the judgment to the mercy behind it.

*And if you were not to receive mercy through Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, our fathers, not one of our seed should be left upon the earth.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 31:4)

That mercy is the fathers'. Before the scattering had begun, Yahuah (the LORD) said that even in the land of their enemies he would remember his covenant.

*Then will I remember my covenant with Jacob, and also my covenant with Isaac, and also my covenant with Abraham will I remember; and I will remember the land.* (Leviticus 26:42)

The cost was walking off the Way, and the mercy was the fathers' covenant that did not let the seed be swept away.$n$,31,1,4,'extras',94040),
('testaments-xii-33-seventy-weeks-and-the-priesthood-of-levi',$n$Seventy weeks, and the priesthood of Levi$n$,$n$Levi pairs the seventy weeks with the priesthood and walks through the priests jubilee by jubilee, the first great, the later ones sorrowful, the seventh full of pollution. Daniel's weeks and the Testament's jubilees are two different things set side by side, and each is read in its own book.

*And whereas ye have heard concerning the seventy weeks, hear also concerning the priesthood.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 33:1)

Daniel sets the weeks upon his people and upon their holy city, and Levi turns straight from the weeks to the priests, who were the keepers of that city.

*Seventy weeks are determined upon thy people and upon thy holy city, to finish the transgression, and to make an end of sins, and to make reconciliation for iniquity, and to bring in everlasting righteousness, and to seal up the vision and prophecy, and to anoint the most Holy.* (Daniel 9:24)

The first priest in Levi's scheme speaks to Elohim (God) as to a father, and Malachi remembers the true priest in the same light, as a man who kept the law of truth and walked with Yahuah in peace.

*The law of truth was in his mouth, and iniquity was not found in his lips: he walked with me in peace and equity, and did turn many away from iniquity.* (Malachi 2:6)

Then the seventh week brings priests who are idolaters, lovers of money, and lawless, and the prophet Malachi had named that same decline in his own day.

*And in the seventh week shall come priests, (who are) idolaters, adulterers, lovers of money, proud, lawless, lascivious, abusers of children and beasts.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 33:11)

*But ye are departed out of the way; ye have caused many to stumble at the law; ye have corrupted the covenant of Levi, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts).* (Malachi 2:8)

In both the failure is that the priests left the way and the law, and the law stands as the measure by which the failure is seen. The bracketed words in the second verse are marked in the text as an addition, and the thread leans on none of them.$n$,33,1,11,'extras',94045),
('testaments-xii-37-strength-against-the-lion-and-the-bear',$n$Strength against the lion and the bear, and whose strength it was$n$,$n$It ain't new. Yahudah (Judah) opens his story of strength by saying Yahuah (the Lord) showed him favour in all his works, and then tells of the hind, the lion, the bear, and the leopard. The feats are his to tell, and the canon had already given the order in which to hear them: the favour first, and the One who gave it.

*And Yahuah (Lord) showed me favour in all my works both in the field and in the house.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 37:1)

The psalmist says it plainly, that no land was won and no deliverance came by their own sword or arm, but by the right hand of Yahuah and the light of his countenance. The strength of the field is a strength given.

*For they got not the land in possession by their own sword, neither did their own arm save them: but thy right hand, and thine arm, and the light of thy countenance, because thou hadst a favour unto them.* (Psalm 44:3)

The tale of the lion and the bear belongs to a long line of such tales, and the best known is David's. When David stood before Saul he spoke of the beasts he had slain and then turned at once to the One who had delivered him out of their paws.

*I slew a lion and plucked a kid out of its mouth. I took a bear by its paw and hurled it down the cliff, and it was crushed.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 37:4)

*David said moreover, Yahuah (LORD) that delivered me out of the paw of the lion, and out of the paw of the bear, he will deliver me out of the hand of this Philistine. And Saul said unto David, Go, and Yahuah (LORD) be with thee.* (1 Samuel 17:37)

Jacob, many years after, blessed this son and chose for him the picture of the lion. The boyhood tale and the father's blessing point at the same man, and the strength was real because the line it ran in had been promised before the tale was told.

*Yahudah (Judah) is a lion’s whelp: from the prey, my son, thou art gone up: he stooped down, he couched as a lion, and as an old lion; who shall rouse him up?* (Genesis 49:9)$n$,37,1,7,'extras',94050),
('testaments-xii-38-the-giant-kings-and-the-angel-that-followed-judah',$n$The giant kings, and the angel that followed Judah$n$,$n$Yahudah (Judah) tells of two Canaanite kings in armour, a giant who hurled javelins from horseback, and a king twelve cubits high whom his father slew, and he ends by saying an angel of might followed him. The scripture beside it measures the giants and names the Angel.

*Achor the king] a man of giant stature 'I found', hurling javelins before and behind as he sat on horseback, and I took up a stone of sixty pounds weight, and hurled it and smote his horse, and killed it.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 38:3)

Jasher tells the stone in the same way, a stone of sixty weight thrown by Judah at a king on a horse. The Testament and the book of Jasher are two memories of one day.

*And Judah took up a large stone from the ground, and its weight was sixty shekels, and Judah ran toward Jashub, and with the stone struck him on his shield, that Jashub was stunned with the blow, and fell off from his horse to the ground.* (Jasher 37:32)

The giants of the Testament are measured against the one giant whose size the Torah gives. Moses said Og alone remained of the remnant of giants, and that his iron bedstead was nine cubits long.

*For only Og king of Bashan remained of the remnant of giants; behold, his bedstead was a bedstead of iron; is it not in Rabbath of the children of Ammon? nine cubits was the length thereof, and four cubits the breadth of it, after the cubit of a man.* (Deuteronomy 3:11)

The chapter ends with the quiet reason the brothers lived through it all. Jacob, who in the Testament dreams of the angel, was the man who at the end of his life spoke of the Angel which redeemed him from all evil.

*The Angel which redeemed me from all evil, bless the lads; and let my name be named on them, and the name of my fathers Abraham and Isaac; and let them grow into a multitude in the midst of the earth.* (Genesis 48:16)$n$,38,3,10,'extras',94055),
('testaments-xii-39-a-thousand-chased-and-hazor-taken',$n$A thousand chased, and Hazor taken$n$,$n$In the south a greater war than the one at Shechem comes upon them, and Yahudah (Judah) tells of pursuing a thousand men, going up on the wall, and taking Hazor. The scripture beside it says who fights for the sons of Yashar'el (Israel).

*And in the south there came upon us a greater war than that in Shechem; and I joined in battle array with my brethren, and pursued a thousand men, and slew of them two hundred men and four kings.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 39:1)

The thousand put to flight is a promise before it is a boast, and Joshua says it to the people in plain words. One man of them should chase a thousand because Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God) was the one fighting for them.

*One man of you shall chase a thousand: for Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God), he it is that fighteth for you, as he hath promised you.* (Joshua 23:10)

Jasher tells the wall in its own account, with Judah first on the top and his brothers following him down into the city.

*And the sons of Jacob came to the city and saw, and behold there was another wall to the city, and they sought for the gate of the wall and could not find it, and Judah sprang upon the top of the wall, and Simeon and Levi followed him, and they all three descended from the wall into the city.* (Jasher 38:14)

Hazor is a city Joshua knew as the head of the kingdoms of the north. The Testament's Hazor falls in the days of the patriarchs and Joshua's in the days of the conquest, and this thread does not settle which is which; it lets the reader see that the promise of the thousand was Yahuah's own.

*And Joshua at that time turned back, and took Hazor, and smote the king thereof with the sword: for Hazor beforetime was the head of all those kingdoms.* (Joshua 11:10)$n$,39,1,3,'extras',94060),
('testaments-xii-40-drawn-down-from-the-wall-and-the-tower-burned',$n$Drawn down from the wall, and the tower burned$n$,$n$Yahudah (Judah) tells of a walled city that threatened them with death, and of a plan that drew the men off the wall while his brothers climbed in on both sides. The plan is an old one in scripture, and so is the fire that ends it.

*And they that were upon the wall, thinking that we were alone, 'were drawn down' against us.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 40:3)

At Ai, Joshua told the ambush to lie behind the city and the people to flee before the men who came out, until the city was drawn open. The Testament's men on the wall, thinking the brothers alone, are drawn down in the same way.

*And I, and all the people that are with me, will approach unto the city: and it shall come to pass, when they come out against us, as at the first, that we will flee before them,* (Joshua 8:5)

Then the brothers take the city and set fire to the tower with the men in it. In Joshua the fire is the commandment of Yahuah (the LORD), a burning the people were told to do, and the Testament tells its tower as a deed of war with no such word given.

*And it shall be, when ye have taken the city, that ye shall set the city on fire: according to the commandment of Yahuah (LORD) shall ye do. See, I have commanded you.* (Joshua 8:8)

Jasher gives the approach in its own telling, with Judah first on the east side of a walled city and his brothers on the other sides, and it tells too of the men of Tapnach coming out to retake the spoil. The fighting does not end with the walls; it goes on over what was carried away.$n$,40,2,7,'extras',94065),
('testaments-xii-41-women-on-the-wall-and-the-waters-of-kozeba',$n$Women on the wall, and the waters of Kozeba$n$,$n$Yahudah (Judah) meets the men of Jobel at the waters of Kozeba, the men of Makir who came to take his spoil, and the women of a city on a hill who rolled stones down upon him. The places and the fights are old, and the canon and Jasher both remember pieces of them.

*And when I was at the waters of Kozeba, the men of 'Jobel' came against us to battle.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 41:1)

Kozeba seems to be the Chezib where Genesis says Shelah was born, a place that belonged to Yahudah's (Judah's) own house. The later list of Shelah's sons ends with *the men of Chozeba* (1 Chronicles 4:22), and the Testament's battle is fought on the ground of his own family.

*And she yet again conceived, and bare a son; and called his name Shelah: and he was at Chezib, when she bare him.* (Genesis 38:5)

The stones from the hill have a long memory too. In scripture a woman on a wall was known to end a king's war with one millstone, and the Testament's women on the brow of the hill did the same to men who came too near.

*And when we came to their city their women rolled upon us stones from the brow of the hill on which the city stood.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 41:4)

*Who smote Abimelech the son of Jerubbesheth? did not a woman cast a piece of a millstone upon him from the wall, that he died in Thebez? why went ye nigh the wall? then say thou, Thy servant Uriah the Hittite is dead also.* (2 Samuel 11:21)

And Jasher tells of a city whose women went out and slung against the sons of Jacob the whole day. The three tellings agree that a city could be defended by women on the wall, and that the brothers did not take it easily.

*And the sons of Jacob fought with them in the city, and all their women were engaged in slinging against the sons of Jacob, and the combat was very severe amongst them the whole of that day till evening.* (Jasher 39:9)$n$,41,1,5,'extras',94070),
('testaments-xii-42-the-gates-opened-by-night-and-thamna-built',$n$The gates opened by night, and Thamna built$n$,$n$Yahudah (Judah) tells of Gaash and its mighty host, of how he and Dan pretended to be Amorites and opened the gates to their brethren by night, of the war at Thamna, and of peace and tribute at the end. Jasher, Jubilees, and Genesis each hold a piece of it.

*I, therefore, and Dan feigned ourselves to be Amorites, and as allies went into their city.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 42:2)

The way in is by a pretending. Scripture knew pretending of this kind and records it from the other side, where it was the men of Yashar'el (Israel) who were deceived.

*They did work wilily, and went and made as if they had been ambassadors, and took old sacks upon their asses, and wine bottles, old, and rent, and bound up;* (Joshua 9:4)

At the end Yahudah (Judah) says he built Thamna and his father built Rabael, and Jubilees remembers the same two places, Robel and Tamnatares, built at the end of the war with the kings of the Amorites.

*And he prevailed over them, and imposed tribute on them that they should pay him tribute, five fruit products of their land, and he built Rôbêl and Tamnâtârês* (Jubilees 34:8)

The reason the cities left them in peace is the one Genesis gives of the sons of Jacob, that the terror of Elohim (God) was upon the cities round about. The fear was Elohim's work and not the brothers'.

*And the Canaanites feared me and my brethren.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 42:11)

*And they journeyed: and the terror of Elohim (God) was upon the cities that were round about them, and they did not pursue after the sons of Jacob.* (Genesis 35:5)$n$,42,1,11,'extras',94075),
('testaments-xii-43-the-adullamite-and-the-daughter-of-shua',$n$The Adullamite, and the daughter of Shua$n$,$n$Yahudah (Judah) turns from war to his household and says he had much cattle, that he went to the Adullamite Iram, that the king of Adullam gave him his daughter at a feast, and that she bare him Er, Onan, and Shelah, two of whom Yahuah (the Lord) smote. Genesis tells the same marriage and the same three sons, in fewer words.

*And when I went to him I saw Barsaba, king of Adullam; 'and he spake unto us', and he made us a feast; and when I was heated he gave me his daughter Bathshua to wife.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 43:2)

Genesis says Yahudah (Judah) saw the daughter of a certain Canaanite, whose name was Shuah, and took her. The two tellings differ on how the match was made, and the thread rests on the simpler one.

*And Yahudah (Judah) saw there a daughter of a certain Canaanite, whose name was Shuah; and he took her, and went in unto her.* (Genesis 38:2)

Isaac's charge to Jacob stood in the house from the beginning, and it is the plain background to a marriage like this.

*And Isaac called Jacob, and blessed him, and charged him, and said unto him, Thou shalt not take a wife of the daughters of Canaan.* (Genesis 28:1)

The Testament comes back to this matter itself in a later chapter, where he speaks of the daughters of Canaan.

*She bare me Er, and Onan, and Shelah; and two of them Yahuah (Lord) smote: for Shelah lived, and his children are ye.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 43:3)

The Chronicles list the same three sons and say they were born of the daughter of Shua the Canaanitess, and that Er was evil in the sight of Yahuah (the LORD). Shelah lived, and the line of Shelah is the line the Testament says is Yahudah's (Judah's) children.

*The sons of Yahudah (Judah); Er, and Onan, and Shelah: which three were born unto him of the daughter of Shua the Canaanitess. And Er, the firstborn of Yahudah (Judah), was evil in the sight of Yahuah (LORD); and he slew him.* (1 Chronicles 2:3)$n$,43,1,3,'extras',94080),
('testaments-xii-44-esau-and-the-arrow-the-iron-city-and-the-tribute',$n$Esau and the arrow, the iron city, and the tribute$n$,$n$Yahudah (Judah) tells of eighteen years of peace between Jacob and Esau, then of Esau coming with a strong people, of the arrow that struck him, of an iron city taken with a ladder, and of peace and tribute until the famine sent them down into Egypt. Genesis and Jubilees hold the shape of the story, and the thread notes where each speaks.

*And eighteen years my father abode at peace with his brother Esau, and his sons with us, after that we came from Mesopotamia, from Laban.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 44:1)

Genesis shows the two brothers side by side at the grave of their father, and nothing in the canon tells of a war after it. The Testament, like Jubilees, tells what came when the peace broke.

*And Isaac gave up the ghost, and died, and was gathered unto his people, being old and full of days: and his sons Esau and Jacob buried him.* (Genesis 35:29)

Jubilees tells the arrow in the same way, as Jacob bent his bow and struck Esau his brother. Genesis says only that Esau dwelt in mount Seir, and the war stands between the burial and the dwelling.

*And Jacob smote Esau with an arrow, and he was taken up wounded on Mount Seir, and as he went he died at Anoniram.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 44:3)

*Then Jacob bent his bow and sent forth the arrow and struck Esau, his brother, (on his right breast) and slew him.* (Jubilees 38:2)

At the end the brothers receive the sons of Esau as tributaries, and Jubilees says Jacob sent word that they should make peace, and they paid tribute to Jacob and to his sons always.

*Then they asked from us terms of peace; and having taken counsel with our father, we received them as tributaries.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 44:7)

*And Jacob sent word to his sons that they should make peace, and they made peace with them, and placed the yoke of servitude upon them, so that they paid tribute to Jacob and to his sons always.* (Jubilees 38:12)$n$,44,1,8,'extras',94085),
('testaments-xii-62-hired-for-the-mandrakes-the-naming-of-issachar',$n$Hired for the mandrakes — how Issachar got his name$n$,$n$It ain't new, because the Testament of Issachar opens with a scene Genesis had already told, and lets the son tell it from his own side, beginning with his birth.

*I was born the 'fifth son to Jacob', by way of hire for the mandrakes.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 62:2)

Genesis shows where the mandrakes came from and who wanted them, and the bargain that followed is the same in both tellings, one night with Jacob paid for in mandrakes.

*And Reuben went in the days of wheat harvest, and found mandrakes in the field, and brought them unto his mother Leah. Then Rachel said to Leah, Give me, I pray thee, of thy son’s mandrakes.* (Genesis 30:14)

*And Jacob came out of the field in the evening, and Leah went out to meet him, and said, Thou must come in unto me; for surely I have hired thee with my son’s mandrakes. And he lay with her that night.* (Genesis 30:16)

*Nevertheless, for the mandrakes I am hiring Jacob to thee for one night.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 62:14)

The name came out of that bargain, and Leah gives the reason in Genesis just as the son repeats it in the Testament.

*And Leah said, Elohim (God) hath given me my hire, because I have given my maiden to my husband: and she called his name Issachar.* (Genesis 30:18)

*And Jacob knew Leah, and she 'conceived and' bare me, and on account of the hire I was called Issachar.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 62:15)

Underneath it lies the wrong done to both sisters, which Rachel names bitterly in the Testament and which Jacob names in Genesis the morning he finds Leah beside him.

*For thou art not his wife, but in craft wert taken to him in my stead.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 62:12)

*And it came to pass, that in the morning, behold, it was Leah: and he said to Laban, What is this thou hast done unto me? did not I serve with thee for Rachel? wherefore then hast thou beguiled me?* (Genesis 29:25)

So the reader is handed no new story but an old one told by a son who remembers how his name was bought. The Testament adds small details, yet the field, the mandrakes, the night, the hire and the name all stand where Genesis put them.$n$,62,2,15,'extras',94090),
('testaments-xii-63-yahuah-hearkened-to-rachel-and-she-bare-two',$n$Yahuah hearkened to Rachel — she was heard, and bare two sons$n$,$n$It ain't new that the barren were heard, for Genesis said it of Rachel and the Testament of Issachar says it again in its own words, telling how Yahuah (the Lord) answered the sister who gave up her night.

*For on the morrow also she again gave up Jacob. Because of the mandrakes, therefore, Yahuah (Lord) hearkened to Rachel.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 63:4)

Genesis puts the answer plainly and leaves the mandrakes out of it.

*And Elohim (God) remembered Rachel, and Elohim (God) hearkened to her, and opened her womb.* (Genesis 30:22)

The Testament also says Rachel wanted children and not pleasure, and Genesis shows the want underneath it in her own cry when the son came.

*For He knew that for the sake of children she wished to company with Jacob, and not for lust of pleasure.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 63:3)

*And she conceived, and bare a son; and said, Elohim (God) hath taken away my reproach:* (Genesis 30:23)

The Testament then has an angel promise the two sons she would bear, while in Genesis her words at Joseph's birth already look ahead to a second.

*Then appeared to Jacob an angel of Yahuah (Lord), saying: Two children shall Rachel bear, inasmuch as she hath refused company with her husband, and hath chosen continency.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 63:1)

*And she called his name Joseph; and said, Yahuah (LORD) shall add to me another son.* (Genesis 30:24)

The reader should notice where the Testament goes beyond Genesis, since it brings an angel to Jacob and carries the mandrakes into the house of Yahuah (the Lord) to a priest of the El Elyon (Most High), and Genesis says nothing of either. The scripture beside it is the record of what was heard: a woman asked, Elohim (God) hearkened, and her reproach was taken away. That is enough to stand on without building anything on the additions.$n$,63,1,5,'extras',94095),
('testaments-xii-81-anger-troubleth-the-soul-and-giveth-place-to-the-devil',$n$Anger troubles the soul, blinds it, and gives the devil a place$n$,$n$It ain't new. Dan's warning about anger runs along lines the scriptures had already drawn, and in the Testament of Dan he speaks it as a dying father who knows what anger does to the one who carries it.

*For anger is an evil thing, my children, for it troubleth even the soul itself.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 81:1)

Proverbs had already named the folly of the hasty spirit, and Dan goes on to show how that folly reasons with itself, with the body doing the deed and the soul calling it just.

*He that is slow to wrath is of great understanding: but he that is hasty of spirit exalteth folly.* (Proverbs 14:29)

*And when the body does all these things, the soul justifieth what is done, since it seeth not aright.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 81:3)

James takes away that comfort in a single line, since however right wrath feels to the angry man it cannot work what Elohim (God) calls righteous.

*For the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of Elohim (God).* (James 1:20)

Dan adds that wrath helps the strong and the weak alike toward lawlessness, which is what Proverbs means when it says a furious man aboundeth in transgression.

*And though the wrathful man be weak, yet hath he a power twofold of that which is by nature; for wrath ever aideth such in lawlessness.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 81:5)

*An angry man stirreth up strife, and a furious man aboundeth in transgression.* (Proverbs 29:22)

Last, Dan sets wrath beside lying at the right hand of Satan, and Ephesians gives a short warning against leaving the devil any place.

*This spirit goeth always with lying 'at the right hand of Satan, that with cruelty and lying' his works may be wrought.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 81:6)

*Neither give place to the devil.* (Ephesians 4:27)

None of this teaches that the commandments are loosened or that a man may be angry and call it righteous. It teaches that anger is a way off the path and a road into lawlessness, and that the one walking it is the last to see it, which is why a father warns his children while they can still turn.$n$,81,1,6,'extras',94100),
('testaments-xii-85-dan-forget-their-elohim-and-alienated-from-the-land',$n$Dan buried near his fathers — yet his sons forget Elohim and are alienated from the land$n$,$n$The Testament of Dan ends with a burial and a sorrow, for his sons lay his bones near the fathers, and then the text adds a prophecy, set in brackets by the translator, of what would come on his seed.

*And his sons buried him. And after that they carried up 'his bones', and placed them near Abraham, and Isaac, and Jacob.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 85:2)

*[Nevertheless, 'Dan' prophesied unto them that they should forget their Elohim (God), and should be alienated from the land of their inheritance 'and from the race of Israel', and from the family of 'their seed'.]* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 85:3)

It ain't new that the scripture said this about Dan, because Moses had already told Yashar'el (Israel) in his song what forgetting looks like, and the book of Judges records how Dan's own children set up their image and kept it until the land went into captivity.

*Of the Rock that begat thee thou art unmindful, and hast forgotten Elohim (God) that formed thee.* (Deuteronomy 32:18)

*And the children of Dan set up the graven image: and Jonathan, the son of Gershom, the son of Manasseh, he and his sons were priests to the tribe of Dan until the day of the captivity of the land.* (Judges 18:30)

The burying place the Testament names is the one Genesis names, the cave Jacob spoke of as his sons gathered, where Abraham and Sarah lay and Isaac and Rebekah after them.

*There they buried Abraham and Sarah his wife; there they buried Isaac and Rebekah his wife; and there I buried Leah.* (Genesis 49:31)

So the Testament of Dan lays Dan beside the fathers and in the same breath grieves that his seed would be separated from the land and from the race of Yashar'el (Israel). That grief is no new teaching, because Moses foretold the forgetting and the later books show it coming true. The bracketed sentence should be held lightly, and the burying place and the song held firmly.$n$,85,2,3,'extras',94105),
('testaments-xii-92-i-believe-elohim-that-joseph-liveth',$n$I believe Elohim that Joseph liveth — Jacob's hope and Naphtali's silence$n$,$n$Naphtali tells his sons of two dreams of Joseph and of the answer his father gave him, and Genesis agrees that Jacob did not forget such dreams.

*These two dreams I told to my father; and he said to me: These things must be fulfilled in their season, after that Yashar'el (Israel) hath endured many things.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 92:1)

*And his brethren envied him; but his father observed the saying.* (Genesis 37:11)

The Testament then shows what Jacob held on to long after the coat was brought, and Genesis keeps both sides of the old man in view, his refusal of comfort and his word when the news came.

*Then my father saith unto me: I believe Elohim (God) that Joseph liveth, for I see 'always' that Yahuah (Lord) numbereth him with you.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 92:2)

*And he said, weeping: Ah me, my son, Joseph, thou livest, though I behold thee not, and thou seest not Jacob that begat thee.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 92:3)

*And all his sons and all his daughters rose up to comfort him; but he refused to be comforted; and he said, For I will go down into the grave unto my son mourning. Thus his father wept for him.* (Genesis 37:35)

*And Yashar'el (Israel) said, It is enough; Joseph my son is yet alive: I will go and see him before I die.* (Genesis 45:28)

Naphtali confesses his own part, and Genesis shows the quiet cost of it, since the father said what he supposed was true.

*He caused me also, therefore, to weep by these words, and I burned in my heart to declare that 'Joseph' had been sold, but I feared my brethren.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 92:4)

*And he knew it, and said, It is my son’s coat; an evil beast hath devoured him; Joseph is without doubt rent in pieces.* (Genesis 37:33)

It ain't new that Jacob grieved and that the brothers kept silent. What the Testament adds is a father who half believed his son still lived and a son who feared to tell him. The two do not agree in every detail, since Genesis shows Jacob's heart faint when first told that Joseph lived, so Genesis is the record and the Testament a father's hope remembered by a son.$n$,92,1,4,'extras',94110),
('testaments-xii-115-woman-reverence-elohim-and-do-not-this-evil-deed',$n$Woman, reverence Elohim — Joseph refuses the threat of poison and the offer of gifts$n$,$n$It ain't new. The temptation Joseph tells his sons about is the one Genesis records, and the commandment he stands on was given before either of them, so that the woman's offer is a breaking of the seventh commandment even as she plans to kill for it.

*'And' again, at another time she said unto me: If thou wilt not commit adultery, I will kill my husband by poison, and take thee to be my husband.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 115:1)

*Thou shalt not commit adultery.* (Exodus 20:14)

Proverbs warned that such a woman hunts for more than a night, and Joseph meets her by telling her whom to fear.

*For by means of a whorish woman a man is brought to a piece of bread: and the adulteress will hunt for the precious life.* (Proverbs 6:26)

*I therefore, when I heard this, rent my garments, and said unto her: Woman, reverence Elohim (God), and do not this evil deed, lest thou be destroyed; for know indeed that I will declare this thy device unto all men.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 115:2)

Genesis says the same in a single sentence, grounding the refusal in Elohim (God) before it is grounded in loyalty to a master.

*There is none greater in this house than I; neither hath he kept back any thing from me but thee, because thou art his wife: how then can I do this great wickedness, and sin against Elohim (God)?* (Genesis 39:9)

When her threat fails she turns to kindness and gifts, and Proverbs describes what that kind of kindness is meant to do.

*And she departed, soothing me with gifts, and sending to me every delight of the sons of men.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 115:4)

*With her much fair speech she caused him to yield, with the flattering of her lips she forced him.* (Proverbs 7:21)

The Testament of Joseph adds a poison plot and an offer of gifts that Genesis does not record, and the reader should keep those as Joseph's own telling. What stands firm is the pattern: the commandment given at Sinai, the warning given in Proverbs, and a young man who answers temptation with the fear of Elohim (God) and does not move. That is Torah lived, not a clever escape from it.$n$,115,1,4,'extras',94115),
('testaments-xii-117-seizing-an-opportunity-she-rushed-unto-me-and-the-cords-of-sin',$n$The woman pleads and threatens, Joseph answers in faithfulness — and the cords of desire$n$,$n$The Testament of Joseph tells the pressure in more detail than Genesis does, for the woman is done with subtlety, and Genesis keeps it short by saying she spoke to him day by day.

*Then accordingly seizing an opportunity she rushed unto me while her husband was yet without, and said unto me: I will hang myself, or cast myself over a cliff, if thou wilt not lie with me.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 117:3)

*And it came to pass, as she spake to Joseph day by day, that he hearkened not unto her, to lie by her, or to be with her.* (Genesis 39:10)

It ain't new that she kept at it, and the Testament simply opens the door a little wider on what those days were. Joseph's answer in the Testament tells whom he was serving, and Genesis gives the reason he gave her himself.

*But she knew not that because of my lord I spake thus, and not because of her.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 117:7)

*But he refused, and said unto his master’s wife, Behold, my master wotteth not what is with me in the house, and he hath committed all that he hath to my hand;* (Genesis 39:8)

The Testament adds a thought about the woman's state, that one given over to desire turns every wise word into fuel for it, and Proverbs describes the same trap from the outside.

*For if a man hath fallen before the passion of a wicked desire and become enslaved by it, even as she, whatever good thing he may hear with regard to that passion, he receiveth it with a view to his wicked desire.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 117:8)

*His own iniquities shall take the wicked himself, and he shall be holden with the cords of his sins.* (Proverbs 5:22)

The woman is bound by her own cords, and Joseph is free because he would not take hold of them. The Testament also says the spirit of Beliar was troubling her and that Joseph prayed to Yahuah (the Lord) when he saw it. That is the Testament's own telling, since Genesis names no spirit, and the reader should not build on it. Genesis and Proverbs give enough: the pressure, the refusal, and the cords that hold a man who gives way.$n$,117,3,8,'extras',94120),
('testaments-xii-122-grace-from-heaven-is-upon-him-bought-into-potiphars-house',$n$Grace from heaven is upon the Hebrew youth — how Joseph came into Potiphar's house$n$,$n$In the Testament of Joseph the Egyptian woman sets the purchase in motion, hearing of the young Hebrew from her eunuchs and going to her husband, while Genesis tells the same event plainly from the husband's side.

*And about that time the Memphian woman, the wife of Pentephri, came down 'in a chariot', 'with great pomp, because she had heard from her eunuchs concerning me'.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 122:1)

*And Joseph was brought down to Egypt; and Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh, captain of the guard, an Egyptian, bought him of the hands of the Ishmeelites, which had brought him down thither.* (Genesis 39:1)

What she says to him is what Genesis says Potiphar saw for himself, and the blessing she promised came.

*Now, therefore, render justice unto him, and take away the youth to thy house; so shall the Elohim (God) of the Hebrews bless thee, for grace from heaven is upon him.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 122:3)

*And his master saw that Yahuah (LORD) was with him, and that Yahuah (LORD) made all that he did to prosper in his hand.* (Genesis 39:3)

*And it came to pass from the time that he had made him overseer in his house, and over all that he had, that Yahuah (LORD) blessed the Egyptian’s house for Joseph’s sake; and the blessing of Yahuah (LORD) was upon all that he had in the house, and in the field.* (Genesis 39:5)

She also says the youth had been stolen out of Canaan, and Joseph said so himself years later.

*And she told her husband that the merchant had become rich by means of a young Hebrew, and they say that he had assuredly been stolen out of the land of Canaan.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 122:2)

*For indeed I was stolen away out of the land of the Hebrews: and here also have I done nothing that they should put me into the dungeon.* (Genesis 40:15)

It ain't new that Yahuah (the LORD) was with Joseph in Egypt. Genesis says it again and again, and the Testament lets even an Egyptian say it. The merchant, the chariot and the court scene are the Testament's own telling. Hold the plain scripture: a son of Yashar'el (Israel) stolen from the land of the Hebrews and blessed in a foreign house because Yahuah (the LORD) was with him.$n$,122,1,3,'extras',94125),
('testaments-xii-123-art-thou-a-slave-or-free-and-i-said-a-slave',$n$Art thou a slave or free? — Joseph is stripped and beaten, and says a slave$n$,$n$The Testament of Joseph lets the reader hear the questioning, with Pentephris asking, the merchant lying and Joseph answering, while Genesis names who really sold him to Potiphar.

*And Pentephris said unto him: Whence, then, is the Hebrew slave? And he said: The Ishmaelites entrusted him to me until they should return.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 123:3)

*And Joseph was brought down to Egypt; and Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh, captain of the guard, an Egyptian, bought him of the hands of the Ishmeelites, which had brought him down thither.* (Genesis 39:1)

Joseph is then asked his own condition, and his answer is short and true, the same as the plain statement in Genesis about how he was sold, which the psalm sums up as a man sold for a servant.

*And he took me apart from him, and said unto me: Art thou a slave or free? And I said: A slave.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 123:6)

*And he said: How didst thou become their slave? And I said: They bought me out of the land of Canaan.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 123:8)

*Then there passed by Midianites merchantmen; and they drew and lifted up Joseph out of the pit, and sold Joseph to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver: and they brought Joseph into Egypt.* (Genesis 37:28)

*He sent a man before them, even Joseph, who was sold for a servant:* (Psalm 105:17)

The Testament has him stripped and beaten for the answer, and the psalm remembers what Joseph suffered.

*And he said unto me: 'Truly' thou liest; and 'straightway' he commanded me to be stripped and beaten.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 123:9)

*Whose feet they hurt with fetters: he was laid in iron:* (Psalm 105:18)

It ain't new that Joseph was sold and afflicted, since Genesis and Psalm 105 had it already. The Testament dramatizes a scene the older books pass over, and it lets Joseph give the answer that spared his brothers shame. It speaks only of Ishmaelites where Genesis also names Midianites, so Genesis 37:28 remains the record. Genesis does not tell this interview, and the Testament is best read beside the verses above and not in place of them.$n$,123,3,9,'extras',94130),
('testaments-xii-124-the-captive-well-born-lad-in-bonds-and-the-word-that-tried-him',$n$The well-born captive in bonds — Joseph imprisoned, and the word that tried him$n$,$n$In the Testament of Joseph the woman watches the beating from her window and pleads that the lad be set free, but Joseph does not change his word and is thrown into prison, a place Genesis also names.

*And when I made no change in my statement, 'though I was beaten', he ordered me to be imprisoned, until, he said, the owners of the boy should come.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 124:2)

*And Joseph’s master took him, and put him into the prison, a place where the king’s prisoners were bound: and he was there in the prison.* (Genesis 39:20)

The Testament is plain about her reason for pleading for him, and Genesis states it at the start, when her desire first appeared.

*For she wished to see me out of a desire 'of sin', but I was ignorant concerning all these things.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 124:4)

*And it came to pass after these things, that his master’s wife cast her eyes upon Joseph; and she said, Lie with me.* (Genesis 39:7)

It ain't new that Joseph was shut up in the king's prison. What the psalm adds is that the prison was a testing, and the Testament of Joseph shows what the testing looked like on the day it began, a beating, a prison, and a lad who did not alter what he said.

*Until the time that his word came: the word of Yahuah (LORD) tried him.* (Psalm 105:19)

The Testament and Genesis differ on how Joseph came to the prison. Genesis puts it after the woman's false charge, when she called the men of her house and accused him, while the Testament has the prison come first, on the husband's doubt about the youth's status, and the reader should see this as the Testament's own account of the same affliction. What holds in both is that a son of Yashar'el (Israel) was bound without cause and that Yahuah (the LORD) was with him there.$n$,124,1,6,'extras',94135),
('testaments-xii-126-i-knew-it-and-held-my-peace-lest-the-eunuch-be-put-to-shame',$n$I knew it and held my peace — Joseph covers the eunuch's shame$n$,$n$The Testament of Joseph tells the buying of Joseph from the Egyptian side, with the woman's eunuchs going back and forth to the Ishmaelites, until the youth comes into her house for a price and the eunuch keeps part of it for himself.

*The eunuch therefore went and gave them eighty pieces of gold, and 'he received me'; but to the Egyptian woman he said: I have given a hundred.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 126:5)

Genesis counts the first sale in silver and names the sellers who passed by, and it tells the sale into Potiphar's house in a single verse.

*Then there passed by Midianites merchantmen; and they drew and lifted up Joseph out of the pit, and sold Joseph to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver: and they brought Joseph into Egypt.* (Genesis 37:28)

*And the Midianites sold him into Egypt unto Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh’s, and captain of the guard.* (Genesis 37:36)

Joseph says in the Testament what he did when he knew the eunuch had cheated, and Proverbs names that restraint for what it is.

*And though I knew (this) I held my peace, lest the eunuch should be put to shame.* (Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 126:6)

*A fool’s wrath is presently known: but a prudent man covereth shame.* (Proverbs 12:16)

It ain't new that a righteous man will keep another's shame hidden rather than take his own advantage. Proverbs had the saying, and the Testament shows a young slave living it before he had any power at all. The details differ from Genesis, which speaks of Midianites and Ishmeelites, twenty pieces of silver and a sale to Potiphar, where the Testament speaks of a mistress, eunuchs, minae and gold, so the two should be kept apart. The value of the passage is that it shows Joseph, a son of Yashar'el (Israel), covering a stranger's fault while he himself was being sold.$n$,126,4,6,'extras',94140);
